# Decisions Log

Append-only, chronological record of non-obvious project decisions: what
was decided, why, and what alternatives were considered. README.md is
the current-state summary; this file is the trail of how we got there.
Every new non-trivial decision gets an entry here, appended at the
bottom, at the time it's made.

Format:

## YYYY-MM-DD — Title
Context: what prompted the decision.
Decision: what was decided.
Why: the reasoning.
Alternatives considered: (optional) what else was on the table and why
it lost.

---

## 2026-08-10 — v1 scope: Ashby scraping + TUI curation only
Context: README's original vision includes multi-board scraping,
scheduled refresh, AI-generated resumes/cover letters, and autonomous
agents.
Decision: this phase covers only Ashby ingestion, storage, fetch-time
filtering, and a TUI to browse/curate what's stored. Everything else is
explicitly deferred, not designed against yet.
Why: keep the data layer and curation workflow solid before building
anything that depends on it (resume generation needs good data first).

## 2026-08-10 — Company config lives in the database, not YAML
Context: README originally specified "configuration via yaml for
interesting companies."
Decision: companies and their filters are stored in the database,
added/edited via the TUI.
Why: user explicitly redirected away from YAML during the PRD
interview toward DB-backed config managed in-app.

## 2026-08-10 — Filter semantics: multi-value OR within a field, AND across fields
Context: needed to decide how multiple filter values combine.
Decision: multiple company_filters rows for the same field (e.g.
department) are OR'd together; distinct fields are AND'd together. A
field with no configured rows imposes no constraint.
Why: matches how a user naturally thinks about "Engineering OR Data
Engineering, AND Remote OR Canada" -- confirmed directly with the user
via concrete examples.

## 2026-08-10 — Filters gate ingestion, not storage queries
Context: whether filtering should happen as a SQL WHERE clause over
stored postings or in application code before storage.
Decision: filter.Match runs in Go, in memory, on postings fetched from
Ashby, before any store write. Non-matching postings are never
inserted.
Why: there's no row to filter yet at decision time -- it's an ingest
gate, not a query. Also keeps the matching logic pure/dependency-free
(microsecond tests, no DB fixture) versus dynamic per-company SQL
generation, which would need runtime WHERE-clause construction per
company's arbitrary filter set.

## 2026-08-10 — Changing a company's filters triggers a full re-fetch
Context: what should happen to already-fetched data when filters
change.
Decision: editing a company's filters triggers a fresh fetch against
current filters.
Why: user's explicit call; keeps filter changes from silently going
stale until the next scheduled/manual refresh.

## 2026-08-10 — Refresh is manual only in v1, no scheduler/daemon
Context: README's long-term vision includes scheduled refresh.
Decision: v1 only supports manual refresh (CLI `swamp fetch`, later
also a TUI keybinding). No background scheduling.
Why: smaller surface area for the first working version; scheduling is
additive later without reshaping anything already built.

## 2026-08-10 — Posting changes are tracked with history, never deleted
Context: how to handle a posting disappearing from a company's board,
or its content changing between fetches.
Decision: postings get a listing_status (open/closed) instead of being
deleted, and content/status changes are recorded as posting_history
snapshots.
Why: preserves markup/notes tied to a posting even after it closes, and
gives a real change trail instead of silently overwriting data.

## 2026-08-10 — Company lifecycle: soft pause only, no hard delete
Context: whether companies should support deletion.
Decision: companies can be paused (stop fetching) but never hard
deleted at the application level.
Why: user's explicit call -- avoids losing postings/markup tied to a
company by accident.

## 2026-08-10 — sqlc + database/sql over an ORM (GORM)
Context: user's prior experience was Rails/ActiveRecord, raised
"why not just use an ORM" directly.
Decision: hand-written SQL + sqlc-generated typed Go, not GORM.
Why: Go's ORM ecosystem doesn't have ActiveRecord's maturity -- GORM
leans on reflection/hidden magic in a language culturally averse to
that (silent N+1s via associations, surprising zero-value handling).
sqlc keeps SQL visible and testable against a real sqlite file, no
runtime query-building layer to debug. Schema here is small enough that
an ORM's abstraction isn't buying much.
Alternatives considered: GORM (more ActiveRecord-like ergonomics, but
less idiomatic and riskier as schema grows).

## 2026-08-10 — goose over golang-migrate for migrations
Context: needed a migration tool to pair with sqlc (which doesn't
handle migrations itself).
Decision: goose, with migration files embedded via go:embed.
Why: simpler/less config than golang-migrate for a solo sqlite-backed
tool, and embeds cleanly into the binary.

## 2026-08-10 — Bubble Tea (+ Bubbles/Lipgloss) for the TUI
Context: needed a TUI framework.
Decision: Bubble Tea, using Bubbles for components (textinput, viewport)
and Lipgloss for styling.
Why: de facto standard for Go TUIs, Elm-architecture model is naturally
testable (Update is a pure function over messages), large component
ecosystem.

## 2026-08-10 — go.mod: minimum-version directive only, no explicit toolchain pin
Context: initially added an explicit `toolchain go1.26.1` line to
go.mod to pin the Go version. `go get`/`go mod tidy` kept silently
stripping it, and a Taskfile task was added to re-pin it after every
tidy.
Decision: removed the re-pinning entirely; go.mod carries only the `go
1.26.1` minimum-version directive, letting Go's own tooling manage the
rest.
Why: user called this out as fighting the tool -- go mod tidy dropping
a redundant toolchain line is intentional design (Go 1.21+ auto-selects
a compatible toolchain from the minimum version), not a bug to patch
around.
Alternatives considered: keep re-pinning via a wrapper Taskfile task
(rejected -- actively worked against the toolchain's own design).

## 2026-08-10 — No ON DELETE CASCADE except tags -> posting_tags
Context: default instinct was to add ON DELETE CASCADE on FKs.
Decision: every FK defaults to SQLite's NO ACTION (delete fails loudly
if dependents exist) except posting_tags.tag_id -> tags, which cascades.
Why: companies/postings are never hard-deleted by the app, so cascades
would only ever fire by accident and could silently wipe owned data
(notes, tags, interview history). The one exception is justified:
deleting a tag you created should remove its associations -- that's an
explicit, expected user action, not a surprise side effect.
Alternatives considered: cascade everywhere (the SQL default reflex;
rejected as exactly the kind of silent-data-loss risk this project
wanted to avoid).

## 2026-08-10 — Ingested vs. owned tables never share columns
Context: risk of naming collisions/confusion between data pulled from a
job board and data the user enters (e.g. a "status" field meaning two
different things).
Decision: postings/posting_history are ingested-only (never written by
the user); posting_markup/tags/posting_tags/interview_stages are
owned-only (never touched by sync). No table mixes both.
Why: user's explicit call for a clean architectural separation, so
there's no ambiguity about which subsystem owns a given column.

## 2026-08-10 — Source-agnostic companies/postings (source + source_ref/source_id)
Context: even though only Ashby is implemented, more job boards are a
stated future goal.
Decision: companies and postings key off (source, source_ref) /
(source, source_id) rather than Ashby-specific fields; postings.
raw_payload preserves the full source response for fields not yet
promoted to canonical columns.
Why: adding a second board later means a new adapter + new rows, not a
schema change. raw_payload means no data is lost even before a field
is canonicalized.

## 2026-08-10 — Soft-delete via deleted_at, not an active boolean
Context: companies originally had an `active` boolean for pause/resume.
Decision: replaced with `deleted_at` (null = active); tags also gained
`deleted_at` instead of supporting hard delete.
Why: on companies, "paused" and "deleted-by-mistake" are different
intents that shouldn't be conflated in one boolean. On tags, soft-delete
means deleting a tag doesn't cascade-destroy historical posting_tags
associations -- a tag's `ON DELETE CASCADE` becomes a fallback the app
shouldn't normally trigger, since the app always soft-deletes.

## 2026-08-10 — created_at/updated_at/deleted_at as a standard convention
Context: these are operational/audit metadata, not business fields.
Decision: applied consistently across owned tables (fixed two gaps:
tags and posting_markup were initially missing full created_at/
updated_at).
Why: consistency, and these fields exist to answer "when did this
happen," not to carry domain meaning.

## 2026-08-10 — gofmt: auto-format pre-commit hook, not just a check
Context: wanted formatting enforced automatically.
Decision: `.githooks/pre-commit` (version-controlled, activated via
`task hooks:install` since `core.hooksPath` can't itself be version
controlled -- .git/config is local, untracked, per-clone state by
git's own design) gofmt's staged .go files and re-stages them before
each commit.
Why: unformatted code should never be possible to commit, not just
flagged after the fact.

## 2026-08-10 — golangci-lint: explicit "standard" linter set, findings fixed not suppressed
Context: first lint run found 3 errcheck findings (unchecked Close()/
Write() errors on defer/cleanup paths) -- a commonly-debated pattern
some teams exclude.
Decision: pinned `linters.default: standard` explicitly in .golangci.yml
(rather than relying on golangci-lint's implicit default, which can
shift between versions) and fixed all 3 findings by explicitly
discarding/asserting on the errors, rather than adding an exclude rule.
Why: user's explicit call to keep the strict default rather than carve
out an exception for a pattern that's rarely actionable but still worth
the discipline.

## 2026-08-11 — Ashby client: verified live API before writing fixtures
Context: had a prior model of the response shape from documentation
summaries only.
Decision: fetched the real public endpoint before writing any decode
code, which corrected two assumptions (field is `descriptionPlain` not
`descriptionText`; there's a top-level `apiVersion`). Confirmed the
endpoint returns all postings in one call, no pagination.
Why: don't build against a remembered/summarized API shape when the
real one is one curl away.

## 2026-08-11 — Ashby decode structs: forgiving types for API evolution
Context: how much resilience to build against future Ashby API changes.
Decision: string types (not enums) for fields like employmentType/
workplaceType, raw JSON preserved via json.RawMessage (byte-exact, not
re-marshaled) per posting. Deliberately did *not* build a pluggable
multi-version API strategy.
Why: additive API changes cost nothing with Go's default
unknown-field-tolerant JSON decoding. A full version-switching
abstraction would be premature -- exactly one API version exists to
design it against.

## 2026-08-11 — filter.Posting is its own minimal type, not ashby.Posting
Context: whether the filter package should depend on ashby's posting
shape.
Decision: filter defines a small local Posting{Department, Location}
type; callers convert at the call site.
Why: keeps filter dependency-free and decoupled from any one board's
client shape (ashby.Posting carries many irrelevant fields --
descriptions, URLs, raw payload). store.Company/postings may become the
real match target once sync exists, so binding to ashby's shape now
would need unwinding later.

## 2026-08-11 — filter.Match: case-insensitive exact match; unsupported field errors
Context: two behavioral choices without an obvious default.
Decision: value comparison is case-insensitive but still exact (not
substring/fuzzy). An unrecognized filter Field returns an error rather
than being silently ignored or treated as never-matching.
Why: job board data casing is inconsistent in practice, so
case-sensitivity would silently miss real matches. On the error path:
filter has no dependency on store's company_filters CHECK constraint,
so it can't assume filters were validated upstream -- fail loud instead
of masking a caller bug (typo'd field, schema/code drift).

## 2026-08-11 — store.UpsertPosting creates the markup row transactionally
Context: every posting should always have exactly one posting_markup
row (default status 'new').
Decision: UpsertPosting wraps the existence check + create/update +
(on insert only) markup creation in one transaction via
Queries.WithTx, rather than requiring callers to remember a second
call.
Why: deep-module preference -- hide the plumbing behind a simple
interface rather than leaking a two-step protocol callers can forget.

## 2026-08-11 — UpsertPosting never touches listing_status
Context: whether re-ingesting a posting's content should implicitly
affect its open/closed state.
Decision: listing_status changes only via explicit
MarkPostingClosed/MarkPostingReopened calls, never as a side effect of
UpsertPosting.
Why: open/closed is a domain transition sync decides deliberately
(based on presence in a fetch), not an incidental side effect of
content refresh.

## 2026-08-11 — Tags soft-delete; list queries differ by purpose
Context: tag deletion needs to not destroy historical tagging data.
Decision: ListTagsForPosting includes soft-deleted tags (a posting's
tag history shouldn't vanish when a tag is retired); ListTags excludes
them (it's the pick-list for applying new tags).
Why: follows directly from why tags soft-delete in the first place --
the two query use cases genuinely want different visibility.

## 2026-08-11 — sync.PostingFetcher: sync's own interface, not ashby's
Context: wanted sync decoupled from any one job board client for
testability and future multi-board support.
Decision: sync defines its own minimal PostingFetcher interface;
*ashby.Client satisfies it structurally.
Why: lets tests use a fake instead of real HTTP. Later found via
self-review that the interface still returns []ashby.Posting
concretely, so it doesn't yet provide true multi-source pluggability --
corrected the doc comment to say so rather than build a speculative
fix with only one real implementation to design against.

## 2026-08-11 — Filter re-match: non-matching postings are left untouched, not kept fresh
Context: if a previously-stored posting stops matching a company's
current filters, should sync still keep its content up to date?
Decision: no -- filters gate whether a posting is touched *at all* on
each fetch, including updates to already-tracked postings. A posting
that stops matching just goes stale (though it's still closed normally
if it disappears from the board, since that check is filter-independent).
Why: literal reading of the README's "non-matching postings are never
stored" -- confirmed directly with the user rather than assumed.
Alternatives considered: keep tracked postings' content fresh
regardless of current filter match (rejected -- inconsistent with the
literal filter contract).

## 2026-08-11 — SyncAll continues past a single company's failure
Context: one company's fetch failing shouldn't block refreshing others.
Decision: SyncAll processes every active company regardless of earlier
failures; each Result carries its own Err.
Why: user's explicit call, and matches how a personal tool should
degrade -- one bad slug or network blip shouldn't stall the whole
batch.

## 2026-08-11 — SyncAll: sequential, not a goroutine fan-out/fan-in pipeline
Context: whether refreshing multiple companies should fetch
concurrently.
Decision: sequential per company.
Why: sqlite writes serialize regardless of fetch concurrency (single
writer without WAL/busy-timeout tuning), so a concurrent pipeline would
mostly just overlap network calls -- real complexity (channels, error
aggregation, non-deterministic test ordering) for a benefit that's
disproportionate at this data scale (a handful of companies). Revisited
directly with the user (who proposed fan-out/collect) and both agreed
sequential is right for now; easy retrofit later since SyncCompany is
already a clean per-company unit.

## 2026-08-11 — Content-change detection: full field comparison, history only on real diff
Context: how to decide when a re-fetched posting counts as "changed."
Decision: compare every ingested field between existing and incoming;
only write a content_updated history snapshot and call UpsertPosting
if something actually differs. An unchanged re-fetch touches the
database not at all.
Why: avoids updated_at churn and pointless writes on a no-op sync,
verified directly (a second identical sync produces an all-zero
Result).

## 2026-08-11 — Posting closure is filter-independent
Context: how to decide when a previously-open posting should be marked
closed.
Decision: any previously-open posting not present in the current
fetch's full (unfiltered) result set gets closed, regardless of
whether it would currently match the company's filters.
Why: a posting disappearing from the board is unconditional fact,
independent of whether this app chose to track it.

## 2026-08-11 — TUI: company management is the first increment, not the full README scope
Context: store initially only supported companies; postings/markup
came later.
Decision: scoped the first TUI increment to company list/add/pause,
deferred posting browsing until store supported it.
Why: matched what was actually buildable at the time rather than
building UI against not-yet-existing data access.

## 2026-08-11 — TUI Update stays side-effect-free; I/O wrapped in tea.Cmd
Context: Bubble Tea's Elm architecture convention.
Decision: every store/sync call happens inside a tea.Cmd, producing a
typed message that Update handles on a later call -- never a direct
call inside Update itself.
Why: this is what makes Update testable without a real terminal: tests
call Update directly, manually execute any returned Cmd, and feed the
result back in.

## 2026-08-11 — TUI list scrolling: custom visibleWindow(), not bubbles/viewport
Context: company/posting lists didn't scroll -- the cursor could move
off-screen with no way to see it.
Decision: a pure visibleWindow(cursor, n, rows) function (centers the
cursor, clamped at the edges) used by both list screens, not
bubbles/viewport.
Why: these are rows of discrete items with their own cursor logic
already built -- viewport is designed for one block of pre-rendered
text, and layering it under an existing cursor system would mean two
overlapping scroll mechanisms instead of one simpler purpose-built one.

## 2026-08-11 — TUI posting detail: bubbles/viewport, unlike the lists
Context: the description text had no scroll mechanism and ran off the
bottom of the terminal.
Decision: the detail body (fields + description) is a viewport.Model;
up/down scroll it. Left/right, previously up/down, now move to the
prev/next posting.
Why: this genuinely is one long scrollable text blob -- exactly
viewport's designed use case, unlike the lists. Freed up/down (the more
natural scroll keys) by moving posting-to-posting navigation to
left/right.

## 2026-08-11 — Browser-open: pure command-selection function, OS process is unverified
Context: needed to open a posting's URL cross-platform (open/xdg-open/
rundll32) without adding a dependency for one exec call.
Decision: split into browserCommand(goos, url) -- a pure function
returning which command/args to use, fully unit tested for all three
platforms plus the unsupported-OS error path -- wrapped by a thin
tea.Cmd that actually spawns the process (not unit tested, same
category as View() rendering).
Why: separates the genuinely testable branching logic from the
actually-untestable-without-a-real-OS side effect, rather than leaving
the whole thing unverified.

## 2026-08-11 — ListPostingsByCompany ordering: added id DESC tiebreaker
Context: user spot-checked posting details against the database and
found apparent mismatches; investigation found ORDER BY first_seen_at
DESC had no tiebreaker, and sqlite's CURRENT_TIMESTAMP has only
second resolution -- a single sync inserts ~130 rows in one loop, so
most share an identical first_seen_at.
Decision: added `id DESC` as a secondary sort key.
Why: verified concretely that ties exist in practice (inserted 3 rows
with identical timestamps, confirmed the query plan uses a temp
b-tree for the sort, not a naturally-ordered scan) -- the observed
order was stable across repeated test runs, but nothing in SQL
semantics guarantees that holds as data volume or the SQLite version
changes. The original mismatch report turned out to not be caused by
this (user later said the bug seemed resolved on its own), but the
missing tiebreaker was a real latent correctness gap worth fixing
regardless.

## 2026-08-12 — Posting detail long lines: word-wrap by default, no toggle, no horizontal scroll
Context: description/URL fields longer than the terminal width ran
off-screen; viewport has latent horizontal-pan support but it's not
wired up (left/right are already claimed for prev/next posting).
Decision: word-wrap content to the viewport's width before
SetContent, re-wrapping on resize. No wrap/raw toggle, no horizontal
scroll.
Why: description text is prose, not tabular/fixed-width content --
wrapping is simply correct for that and needs no new keybinding to
discover. A toggle would add a mode for a need (viewing raw
fixed-width content) that doesn't exist in this data. Horizontal
scroll is the right tool only for genuinely wide fixed-format content,
which doesn't apply here; noted as an easy option to add later
without conflicting with this if that ever changes.
Alternatives considered: wrap/raw toggle keybinding; horizontal
scroll via viewport's native pan (would require moving posting
navigation off left/right).

## 2026-08-12 — Decision history: decisions.log, backfilled from project start
Context: wanted traceable history of project decisions beyond what's
implicit in commit messages.
Decision: this file. Append-only, one entry per non-trivial decision,
going forward from now -- and, per explicit request, backfilled for
the major decisions already made this session rather than starting
empty.
Why: commit messages capture the "why" for what shipped, but don't
give a single browsable chronological trail across the whole project,
and can get lost/summarized away in a squash or rebase. This file is
the durable record independent of git history shape.

## 2026-08-12 — Word-wrap implementation: cellbuf.Wrap directly, not lipgloss.Style.Width()
Context: implementing the word-wrap decision above.
Decision: wrapToWidth uses github.com/charmbracelet/x/cellbuf's Wrap
function directly, not lipgloss.NewStyle().Width(w).Render(). Also:
re-wrap explicitly on every tea.WindowSizeMsg while viewing detail,
by re-calling showPostingDetail() (which re-wraps from the posting's
original content), rather than just updating detailViewport.Width.
Why: two things were verified empirically, not assumed. First,
lipgloss's Width() also pads short lines to fill the width (it's a
box-layout primitive, not pure text wrapping) -- confirmed by a
failing test expecting unchanged short content. Second,
viewport.View() renders via lipgloss MaxWidth, which *truncates*
content wider than the current Width rather than re-wrapping it --
so just resizing detailViewport.Width on a window resize would
silently cut off text instead of reflowing it. Confirmed with a test
that specifically checks a marker word survives a resize (a
weaker "no line exceeds width" check alone doesn't catch truncation,
since truncated lines also satisfy that property).

## 2026-08-12 — Vim keys (h/j/k/l) added alongside arrow keys, not replacing them
Context: wanted vim-style navigation throughout the TUI.
Decision: j/k added as equivalents to down/up on both list screens;
h/l added as equivalents to left/right for prev/next-posting in the
detail view. Arrow keys keep working unchanged. Scrolling in the
detail view needed no changes at all -- verified that
bubbles/viewport's DefaultKeyMap already binds j/k/h/l alongside the
arrow keys.
Why: additive, not a replacement -- matches near-universal convention
in vim-inspired TUIs (lazygit, k9s, fzf, less all support both), and
there's no real cost to keeping arrows working for anyone who doesn't
use vim bindings. No key collisions with existing single-letter
bindings (a/p/r/o/q/b).

## 2026-08-12 — Filter values: strings from our public API, not Ashby's internal IDs
Context: Ashby's own careers-page UI (jobs.ashbyhq.com/{company}) updates
its URL with ID-based params (e.g. locationId=<uuid>) when filtering,
raising the question of whether we should filter on IDs instead of
department/location strings for robustness against renames.
Decision: keep filtering on the plain-string department/location values
already in our public API response. Did not pursue scraping Ashby's
careers-page HTML for ID data.
Why: verified two things empirically before deciding. First, the public
API we use (api.ashbyhq.com/posting-api/job-board/{slug}) has no ID
fields at all for department/location/team -- IDs only appear in a
<script>-embedded JSON blob inside the HTML of Ashby's own careers page,
which is undocumented frontend implementation detail, not a stable
contract, and could change silently on any Ashby deploy. Second, even
that embedded data doesn't line up with our fields: it has teamId and
locationId, no departmentId, so it's not even clear which of our fields
(department vs team) an ID would correspond to -- and there's no visible
ID-to-display-name lookup table to build a usable picker from anyway
(just opaque UUIDs). Building an HTML-scraping fallback for a data
source with unclear field mapping and no display names, alongside the
clean documented-API client we already have, wasn't worth it.

## 2026-08-12 — TUI filter selection: narrow immediately, reload authoritatively after re-sync
Context: selecting/saving a company's department/location filters from
the posting list needed a UX decision -- does the visible list narrow
right away, or only after the next sync actually changes what's stored
(the existing non-retroactive filter semantics)?
Decision: on save, apply filter.Match client-side against the
already-loaded postings for instant visual feedback, then kick off a
background SyncCompany re-fetch (existing "filter change triggers
re-fetch" behavior). When that sync completes, if the refreshed
company is the one currently being viewed (matched by
sync.Result.CompanyID), reload postings from the DB so the view
becomes authoritative instead of staying with just the optimistic
client-side narrowing.
Why: confirmed directly with the user rather than assumed -- pure "only
affects future syncs" would leave the view showing everything until a
manual refresh, which doesn't match "select a filter from the list and
see it take effect" intent. The two-step (optimistic narrow, then
authoritative reload) gets both instant feedback and correctness
without needing the view-time SQL filtering that would otherwise be
needed to keep company_filters' existing ingest-time-only semantics
intact.

## 2026-08-12 — Filter value keyspace: two explicit queries, not a dynamic-field one
Context: department and location values are company-specific
vocabularies (Ramp's departments aren't any other company's), so
filter options need to come from what's actually been ingested.
Decision: ListDistinctDepartmentsForCompany/
ListDistinctLocationsForCompany as two separate sqlc queries.
Why: matches company_filters' existing field IN ('department',
'location') CHECK constraint, and sqlc doesn't support a bind
parameter as a column name anyway (no dynamic-field query is even
possible without hand-rolled SQL string building). Trivial to extend
with a third explicit query if another filterable field is ever
added.

## 2026-08-12 — loadPostings re-applies saved filters after every fetch
Context: found via user bug report ("after entering a filter the
posting list does not seem to update") that the optimistic client-side
narrow on save was getting silently undone. Root cause: the background
re-sync's completion triggers a reload via loadPostings ->
ListPostingsByCompany, which has no notion of company_filters at all
(filters gate ingestion only, a deliberate earlier decision) -- so the
"authoritative reload" was authoritatively wrong, loading everything
unfiltered and overwriting the narrowed view.
Decision: loadPostings now also fetches the company's current
company_filters and re-applies them (via the same narrowPostingsToFilters
used for the optimistic narrow) to every set of postings it returns,
not just the one right after a save.
Why: this is the one place postings get loaded for display (both first
entry into the posting list and every reload after), so making it
filter-aware there covers both the immediate bug and, for free, true
cross-run persistence -- a company with filters saved from a prior
session now narrows on first load too, not just right after saving in
the same session. Caught by extending the existing save-flow test to
drive through the full save -> sync -> reload chain instead of only
checking the immediate optimistic-narrow state; a second test confirms
first-load narrowing for filters that already existed before the
session started (not just just-saved ones), since that's the literal
"persisting across runs" requirement and wasn't otherwise exercised.

## 2026-08-14 — application lifecycle expansion, take 1: enum-only (superseded)
Context: user wants to track application progress (interested ->
application started -> submitted -> interviewing (loops) -> offer
received/accepted/declined) beyond the original 6-value
posting_markup.user_status enum, partly to support future coordination
with external services (e.g. triggering cover-letter/resume generation
off application state).
First attempt: just widen posting_markup.user_status's CHECK constraint
to the new value set. Built (migration 00002, tests, all green) but
scrapped before merging -- see next entry.

## 2026-08-14 — split Application out of posting_markup as its own entity
Context: mid-build of the enum-only change above, recognized (user
prompted "I think we are conflating job postings with job applications")
that a posting (Ashby's ingested listing, own lifecycle: open/closed,
tracked in postings.listing_status) and an application (the user's own
pursuit of that posting) are different artifacts with different states.
posting_markup was originally the user's only markup surface and had
absorbed both concerns onto one 1-row-per-posting table.
Decision: split into two tables/entities.
- posting_markup keeps a narrow, always-present, posting-level triage
  status: new / interested / archived. No application-specific values.
- New applications table holds the pursuit lifecycle: application_started
  -> application_submitted -> interviewing -> rejected, and
  interviewing -> offer_received -> offer_accepted / offer_declined.
  Not auto-created (unlike posting_markup) -- only exists once the user
  takes an explicit "start application" action.
- interview_stages now hangs off application_id, not posting_id (an
  interview round belongs to a specific application attempt).
Cardinality: applications.posting_id is a PRIMARY KEY (1:1), same
pattern as posting_markup, not a plain FK. Considered 1:many to support
reapplying to a posting that closes and reopens under the same
source_id (confirmed possible, since MarkPostingReopened already
assumes source_id stability across a close/reopen cycle) but decided
against it for now: every read path (TUI, sync, store) would need a
"which application is current" resolution rule for a case with zero
observed occurrences so far, and 1:1 -> 1:many later is a cheap,
additive migration (drop the PRIMARY KEY for a surrogate id), whereas
building 1:many now and finding it unneeded would mean unwinding
"pick the current one" logic spread across every call site. If/when
reapplication is actually needed, will revisit then.
Migration (00002_split_application_from_posting.sql) backfills:
existing posting_markup rows with user_status in
(applied/interviewing/rejected) produce a corresponding applications
row (translated to application_submitted/interviewing/rejected) and
posting_markup's own status narrows to 'interested'; existing
interview_stages rows re-point to the backfilled application.
Existing posting_markup.notes deliberately stay on posting_markup as-is
rather than being guessed apart between posting- and application-level
notes.
Not yet done: wiring any of this into the TUI (no keybinding exists yet
to start an application, change its status, or view it) -- schema and
store layer only, same pattern as the original interview_stages build.

## 2026-08-14 — applications gets a real surrogate id, not posting_id reused
Context: initial split (previous entry) gave applications the same
"reuse the parent's id as PK" shape as posting_markup
(posting_id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY). User caught it during review ("check
your IDs, PostingID mixed up with ApplicationID"): unlike posting_markup,
applications is now itself a parent table (interview_stages references
it), so reusing posting_id as its identity meant
interview_stages.application_id was storing posting_id values under an
application_id name, and code like CreateInterviewStage(ctx,
application.PostingID, ...) read as passing the wrong field even though
the value happened to be numerically correct today.
Decision: applications gets its own surrogate id (id INTEGER PRIMARY
KEY, posting_id INTEGER NOT NULL UNIQUE) instead. interview_stages.
application_id now references applications(id), matching the
established pattern elsewhere in the schema (interview_stages
originally referenced postings(id), not postings' own natural key).
Caught before merge, not after -- migration, sqlc queries, store layer,
and both test files updated together; no shipped data affected.

## 2026-08-14 — applications.posting_id: 1:1, not 1:many (expanded rationale)
Context: the previous entry's "Cardinality" paragraph summarizes this
tersely; expanding here since it was a real fork with a full pros/cons
discussion, not an obvious call. Prompted by user question: "stop and
think about my questions on is this a one to one or a many to one
relationship. What are the cons of representing as a one to many
relationship in the near term?"
The scenario motivating 1:many: a posting can close and later reopen
under the same source_id (sync already supports this via
MarkPostingReopened), so in principle a user could apply, get rejected,
and later reapply when the same req reopens -- two genuinely distinct
application attempts against one posting row.
Cons of building 1:many now, weighed before deciding:
- Every read path that looks up "the application for this posting"
  (TUI posting detail, sync, any future coordination hook) would need
  a "which one is current" rule -- e.g. most-recent-by-created_at --
  for a case with zero observed occurrences so far.
- interview_stages inherits the ambiguity: "show interview stages for
  this posting" would need to pick an application first if more than
  one exists.
- No real signal yet that reapplication is something a user actually
  wants captured as two independent records rather than just resetting
  status on the existing application when they choose to reapply.
- Direction of cheap correction is asymmetric: 1:1 -> 1:many later is
  additive (drop a UNIQUE constraint); building 1:many now and finding
  it unneeded means unwinding "pick the current one" logic spread
  across every call site that would have accumulated in the meantime.
Decision: 1:1 (posting_id UNIQUE on applications) for now, per
CLAUDE.md's "need 3 real examples before abstracting" standard --
reopening-with-reapplication is a single hypothetical scenario, not an
observed need. If a posting does reopen while an old application
exists, handle it as an explicit user decision at that moment (reset
the existing application, or revisit the schema then) rather than
pre-building for it.

## 2026-08-14 — posting_markup.user_status replaced with interested_at/archived_at flags
Context: user pushed back on the earlier new/interested/archived enum
("we are getting ahead of ourselves ... interested should be a separate
boolean used for filtering"). "Interested" isn't a pipeline stage --
it's an independent bookmark/tag for later review, and you don't
"leave" being interested the way an application progresses through
states. Use case: mark postings for later review/application, or
quick filtering.
Decision: dropped the CHECK-constrained enum entirely. posting_markup
now has interested_at TIMESTAMP NULL and archived_at TIMESTAMP NULL,
independently settable (IS NOT NULL doubles as the boolean check).
archived_at mirrors the deleted_at soft-delete convention already used
for companies and tags elsewhere in this schema, rather than
introducing a new idiom. Both can be set at once now (e.g. archiving
something you were once interested in) -- the old single-enum model
would have silently lost the "was interested" fact the moment
something was archived; this doesn't.
Considered and rejected two alternatives (user asked for the
comparison directly):
- Plain boolean instead of timestamp: strictly worse here -- loses
  "when," which is directly useful for sorting/filtering a watchlist
  by recency, and buys nothing over a nullable timestamp since
  IS NOT NULL already gives the boolean check for free.
- Separate append-only events table (posting_id, event_type,
  occurred_at), mirroring posting_history's existing pattern for
  ingested-content changes: gives full toggle history, but "am I
  currently interested" becomes a latest-per-type lookup instead of a
  column check -- real cost on the TUI posting-list filter hot path --
  for a history requirement that doesn't exist yet. Same "need 3 real
  examples before abstracting" reasoning as the applications
  cardinality decision. posting_history is direct prior art in this
  codebase for revisiting this if a real need for full history shows
  up later; not a one-way door.
Store API: MarkPostingInterested/UnmarkPostingInterested and
ArchivePosting/UnarchivePosting (mirrors posting.go's
MarkPostingClosed/MarkPostingReopened naming), replacing
UpdatePostingMarkupStatus.

## 2026-08-14 — CreateCompany restores instead of erroring on re-add of a paused company
Context: companies.(source, source_ref) is UNIQUE, and the constraint
applies regardless of deleted_at (see "Company lifecycle: soft pause
only, no hard delete", 2026-08-10). Pausing a company via the TUI's
"p" key (Store.SoftDeleteCompany) removes it from the active list but
leaves the row in place. Re-adding the same company (TUI "a" form,
same source_ref) previously hit CreateCompany's plain INSERT and
failed with a raw UNIQUE constraint violation instead of doing
anything sensible -- confirmed via a failing test before the fix.
Decision: CreateCompany now looks up an existing row by (source,
source_ref) first (ignoring deleted_at) inside a transaction; if
found, it restores that row (clears deleted_at) and updates the name,
rather than attempting a duplicate insert. Mirrors the existing
UpsertPosting pattern (find-by-natural-key, insert-or-update in one
tx) rather than inventing a new shape. No new sentinel error --
callers of CreateCompany don't need to distinguish "created" from
"restored," and the TUI's existing companyCreatedMsg handling already
does the right thing (appends to the active list) since a paused
company isn't in that list to begin with.
Alternative considered: return a distinct "already exists" error and
make the caller decide. Rejected -- there's no case in this app today
where silently restoring is the wrong call (the paused company's
postings/markup/applications were never deleted, so there's nothing
to reconcile), and it avoids a UI-side prompt for a decision the user
already made by re-adding.

## 2026-08-14 — Company list keybinding renamed p (pause) to d (delete)
Context: the underlying behavior (SoftDeleteCompany) hasn't changed --
still soft-delete only, per "Company lifecycle: soft pause only, no
hard delete" (2026-08-10). But issue #1 asked for a way to "delete a
company from the company view," and the "p: pause" label didn't read
as that action, so the ask wasn't discoverable even though the
capability already existed.
Decision: renamed the keybinding from "p" to "d" and relabeled it
"delete" in the help text, plus the internal names that shadowed it
(pauseCompany -> deleteCompany, companyPausedMsg -> companyDeletedMsg)
so code and UI say the same thing. Purely a naming change -- still a
soft delete under the hood, nothing about the 2026-08-10 decision
changed.

## 2026-08-14 — Posting list markup keybindings (i/x) and marker rendering
Context: issue #2 -- store already had MarkPostingInterested/
ArchivePosting etc. (see the interested_at/archived_at decision
above) but nothing in the TUI called them.
Decision: "i" toggles interested, "x" toggles archived, on the
posting under the cursor in the posting list. Each posting's markup
is loaded alongside the posting list itself (loadPostings now also
calls GetPostingMarkup per posting -- N+1 against local sqlite, cheap
enough not to warrant a join query yet) and rendered as a
single-character marker column (★ interested, ✕ archived, blank
neither). When both flags are set, the marker shows archived --
matches the precedence the 00003 migration's Down path already uses
when collapsing both back into one enum value, so the two places that
have to pick a single representation agree with each other.

## 2026-08-14 — i/x keybindings switch state instead of stacking it
Context: manual testing of the above turned up a confusing case --
schema allows interested_at and archived_at to be independently set,
but the TUI's "i"/"x" keys as first built only ever set/cleared their
own flag. Pressing "i" while a posting was already archived set
interested_at without clearing archived_at, so the marker (which
shows archived when both are set, per the entry above) didn't appear
to change -- looked like the keypress did nothing.
Decision: "i" and "x" now behave as an exclusive switch from the
user's perspective: pressing one while the other is set clears the
other and sets the pressed one (new SetPostingInterested/
SetPostingArchived queries, each a single UPDATE setting one
timestamp and NULLing the other). Pressing the same key that's
already exclusively active still toggles it off back to neutral
(unchanged, via the existing UnmarkPostingInterested/
UnarchivePosting). The schema itself is untouched -- both columns can
still independently be NULL/non-NULL -- this is TUI-level behavior
only, layered on top via two new combined-update queries rather than
composing two separate calls, to keep each keypress a single atomic
UPDATE.

## 2026-08-15 — Dev tools (golangci-lint, sqlc, goose, task) pinned via go.mod tool directives
Context: the four dev tools were installed ad hoc into a gitignored
`bin/` via direnv's GOBIN, with no version recorded anywhere in the
repo -- fine for one machine, but meant a CI setup or a new clone had
nothing to reproduce the exact versions from. This came up directly
while adding CI (needing lint/test to run against the same tool
versions as local dev).
Decision: all four are now `go.mod` tool dependencies (Go 1.24+'s
`tool (...)` directive, added via `go get -tool <path>@<version>`),
invoked as `go tool <name>` instead of `./bin/<name>`. `go.mod`/
`go.sum` are now the single source of truth for tool versions -- a
fresh clone or CI needs nothing beyond `go` itself; the pinned binary
builds/caches automatically on first `go tool` invocation. Removed
`.envrc`'s `GOBIN`/`PATH_add bin` (nothing installs into `bin/` this
way anymore -- `task build` still writes `./bin/swamp`, but `go build
-o` creates that directory itself, confirmed by testing against a
renamed-away `bin/`). The full local verification checklist (build/
test/lint/fmt:check) no longer needs `direnv exec .` at all -- none of
those four commands depend on `SWAMP_DB_PATH` (confirmed by running
`go test ./...` with `SWAMP_DB_PATH`/`GOBIN` unset); direnv's only
remaining job is `SWAMP_DB_PATH` for actually running the app.
Accepted tradeoff: golangci-lint specifically adds a large `go.sum`
diff (~900 lines) when pinned this way, because it bundles all its
linters as library dependencies rather than being a single lean
binary. Considered keeping golangci-lint on a separate install path
(official install script/binary release) to avoid that bloat while
sqlc/goose/task (much lighter) still used `go tool` -- rejected in
favor of one uniform mechanism for all four tools rather than two
different ones to remember and document.

## 2026-08-15 — CI runs the actual Taskfile commands via go tool, fmt not lint
Context: CI was first built against golangci-lint-action + raw `go
test`, before the go.mod tool-directive conversion above existed --
so local dev (`task lint`/`task test`) and CI used two different
mechanisms to reach the same result, with separate version pinning
(golangci-lint-action's `version:` input vs whatever was in `bin/`).
User explicitly asked to keep CI and the Taskfile workflow the same
where possible, and to check fmt instead of lint for now.
Decision: rebuilt on top of the go.mod tool-directive conversion --
CI (.github/workflows/ci.yml) now runs `go tool task test` and
`go tool task fmt:check`, the identical commands used locally, no
separate action or version pin needed since go.mod/go.sum already
pin every tool `task` might invoke. Dropped the lint job entirely
(explicit user call, not an oversight) -- golangci-lint-action is
gone, and since `go tool task test`/`fmt:check` never invoke
golangci-lint, CI doesn't pay golangci-lint's large dependency-build
cost either. Verified both commands pass locally with
`SWAMP_DB_PATH` unset, matching what a bare CI runner will have.
Lint can be added back later as `go tool task lint` the same way, if
wanted.

## 2026-08-15 — Hide-archived posting filter: ephemeral, posting-list keybinding, hidden by default
Context: issue #11 -- archived postings (interested_at/archived_at
flags, see the 2026-08-14 entry above) had no way to be filtered out
of the main posting list.
Decision: a new `A` keybinding on the posting list toggles
`App.hideArchived` (defaults true) and reloads via loadPostings,
which now drops archived postings from the result when hideArchived
is set. Deliberately NOT a company_filters row -- that table's
semantics are ingestion-gating (ashby fetch time, evaluated per
company), while this is a pure display concern, evaluated after
fetch on data already in the DB. Deliberately NOT persisted (no new
table/column) -- ephemeral in-memory App state only, per "3 real
examples before abstracting" (same reasoning as the interested_at/
archived_at boolean-vs-timestamp decision) -- trivial to add
persistence later by seeding the initial value from a settings row
instead of hardcoding true, without touching the filtering logic.
Considered folding this into screenFilterSelect alongside Department/
Location (closer to the issue's original phrasing) -- rejected in
favor of a dedicated keybinding matching the existing i/x pattern,
since that screen's save flow persists to company_filters and this
toggle deliberately doesn't, and mixing persisted/ephemeral state in
one screen's cursor+save model added complexity for no real benefit.
Consistency follow-on: archiving a posting (`x`) while hideArchived
is on now also removes it from the in-memory posting list
immediately (with cursor clamping), rather than leaving it visible
until the next reload -- otherwise "hide archived" wouldn't actually
hide postings archived during the current session. This also means
unarchiving a posting requires pressing `A` first to reveal it again,
since it's no longer reachable by cursor once hidden -- an accepted,
intentional consequence, not a bug (updated two existing tests that
assumed archived postings stayed cursor-reachable).

## 2026-08-19 — Wire up application lifecycle in the TUI (issue #4): full-enum status select, textarea notes, a/s/n on posting detail
Context: store/application.go (CreateApplication, GetApplication,
UpdateApplicationStatus, UpdateApplicationNotes) has been fully
implemented and tested for a while, but none of it was reachable from
the TUI -- no way to start an application, see its status, change it,
or record notes without leaving the app.
Decision: three new posting-detail keybindings -- 'a' starts an
application (CreateApplication) if the selected posting doesn't have
one yet; 's' opens a new screenApplicationStatusSelect screen (cursor
list, same shape as screenFilterSelect) listing all seven
applications.status enum values unconditionally; 'n' opens a new
screenApplicationNotesEdit screen (bubbles/textarea, pre-populated
with existing notes) with ctrl+s to save (UpdateApplicationNotes) and
esc to cancel. Posting detail now also renders the application's
current status/notes (or "No application started -- press 'a' to
start one." when none exists), sourced from a new
App.applicationsByPosting map[int64]store.Application populated by an
async loadApplication command dispatched whenever detail is entered
or navigated between postings (h/l) -- postingDetailContent stays a
pure function of already-loaded state, per the package's Update
side-effect-free convention; the fetch itself happens in the Cmd, not
inside the render path.
Why a full-enum select instead of a hardcoded "valid next status"
transition graph: the applications.status column's CHECK constraint
(db/migrations/00002_split_application_from_posting.sql) allows any
of the seven values from any row state -- the schema encodes no
transition graph at all. Hardcoding one in the TUI would be inventing
a state machine the DB doesn't have and would need to be kept in sync
by hand for no benefit yet -- YAGNI until an actual need for enforced
transitions shows up (e.g. an offer_accepted -> application_started
regression makes no real-world sense, but nothing today says users
won't want to correct a mis-click that way).
Why textarea instead of textinput for notes: application.Notes is a
free-form multi-line TEXT column (unlike the company form's
single-line name/source-ref fields, which use textinput). bubbles/
textarea was already available transitively via the pinned
charmbracelet/bubbles v1.0.0 dependency -- no go.mod change needed.
Enter inserts a newline in textarea (its default keymap), so save
couldn't reuse Enter the way every other screen in this app does;
ctrl+s was chosen as an unambiguous, uncommon-enough-to-not-collide
save keybinding, with esc kept as cancel for consistency with every
other screen's cancel key.
Key choices: 'a'/'s'/'n' scoped to screenPostingDetail only (not
posting list) -- keeps the interaction anchored to the screen that
now displays application state, and avoids duplicating the
existence-check logic across two screens for no clear benefit yet.
Confirmed lowercase 'a' was unbound in that screen before this change
(only uppercase 'A', a posting-list-only archived-visibility toggle,
was in use -- different screen, no collision). 'a' pressed a second
time when an application already exists is a deliberate no-op (guard
checked against the in-memory applicationsByPosting map before
calling CreateApplication) rather than a store-level error path,
since posting_id is UNIQUE on the applications table and a second
CreateApplication call would otherwise hit that constraint.
Verification: full TDD suite (go test ./tui/... -v, one test at a
time) plus a throwaway harness (not committed) that drove the real
tui.App through New/Update/View exactly as Bubble Tea's runtime would,
against a real goose-migrated scratch sqlite db -- seeded a company/
posting, then walked company list -> posting list -> posting detail
-> create application -> status select -> set status -> notes editor
-> save notes -> back to list, printing View() at each step and cross-
checking final store state (GetApplication) against what was typed.
Noted overlap risk: a separate concurrent change (issue #5, cover
letter/resume document storage, different worktree/branch) is also
adding an App field and an async GetApplication fetch on entering
posting detail, purely to detect document-file presence -- likely to
collide with applicationsByPosting/loadApplication here at PR-review/
rebase time. Not resolved here; flagged for Dana to reconcile when
both PRs are ready.

## 2026-08-19 — Application status becomes a Go enum; DB CHECK constraint dropped (PR #16 review)
Context: Dana's inline review on PR #16 (tui/app.go line 34,
comment id 3813059905): "this should be using a const iota pattern to
simulate an enum implementing the stringer interface. We can mark/
drop the constraint in the database and have the check here in code
vs downstream in the DB. Duplicating the check doesn't hold value
especially the added head ache of keeping them in sync." Prior to
this, application status was a bare string everywhere (store.
Application.Status, UpdateApplicationStatus's parameter, the TUI's
applicationStatuses []string) with the only real enforcement living
in the applications.status CHECK constraint (00002) -- exactly the
duplication called out.
Decision: added store.ApplicationStatus (store/application_status.go)
-- a named int type, const iota values (ApplicationStatusStarted ...
ApplicationStatusOfferDeclined), a hand-written String() (no stringer
codegen tool added to go.mod for 7 values -- not worth it), and
ParseApplicationStatus for the DB-string -> enum direction. store is
the right home over tui or store/db: store/db is sqlc-generated and
will always produce plain `string` for a SQLite TEXT column regardless
of anything else, and store.Application is the domain type postings/
applications already live on. Application.Status, UpdateApplication
Status's parameter, and applicationFromRow (the sqlc-row -> domain-
struct boundary) all switched to the typed value; applicationFromRow
now returns (Application, error) since parsing can fail. tui/app.go's
applicationStatuses var now aliases store.ApplicationStatuses()
instead of maintaining its own []string copy, and applicationStatusIndex
takes the typed value.
Enforcement moved, not removed: ParseApplicationStatus is the one
place a raw DB string is validated on the way in, and fails loudly
(returns an error, not a silent default) for anything outside the
known set -- this is what now stands in for the dropped DB CHECK.
Migration 00004 (db/migrations/00004_drop_application_status_check_
constraint.sql) rebuilds applications without the CHECK, following
the same _new/copy-data/drop/rename pattern 00002 and 00003 used
(SQLite has no ALTER TABLE ... DROP CONSTRAINT); Down restores the
original 00002 constraint, verified to round-trip correctly (manual
Up->Down->re-insert-invalid-value check, not added as a checked-in
test since no prior migration in this repo has Down-path test
coverage either -- staying consistent rather than introducing a new
convention for one migration).
Test fallout: store/application_test.go's
TestUpdateApplicationStatus_RejectsRemovedAppliedValue asserted the
DB CHECK constraint rejected "applied" -- can no longer compile once
the parameter is typed (a string literal doesn't convert to
ApplicationStatus). Removed; the same intent is now covered by
store/application_status_test.go's ParseApplicationStatus rejection
test, at the layer that actually enforces it post-change. Several
other tests (store and tui) that compared .Status against bare string
literals were updated to compare against the typed constants instead
-- expected fallout from a type change, not a smell (see CLAUDE.md
Testing section on TDD covering behavior, and the task instructions
for this PR-review round explicitly calling this out as expected).
Re-ran the same manual tophat harness used for the original PR #16
work (throwaway, not committed) end-to-end after this change and
confirmed byte-identical rendered output to before -- ApplicationStatus.
String() produces the same DB-format strings the old []string literal
did, so this is a pure internal refactor with no user-visible
behavior change.
Landed as its own PR (application-status-enum, stacked on top of
wire-up-application-lifecycle/#16), not bundled into #16 -- Dana's
call, made right after the review comment above: #16 should stay
scoped to the original application-lifecycle feature it was already
reviewed against, with the enum/constraint-drop as an isolated,
separately-reviewable diff on top rather than silently expanding #16
after review had already started. The two commits implementing this
(fc0758f, 8f2c175) had not been pushed yet when the split was
requested, so no force-push/history-rewrite was needed -- just a new
branch pointer plus a hard reset of wire-up-application-lifecycle back
to what origin already had.

## 2026-08-19 — applications.status also drops NOT NULL/DEFAULT; DB doesn't invent the initial value either (PR #17 review)
Context: follow-up review comment on PR #17 (db/migrations/00004_...
line 17, comment id 3813210549), on the line just landed in the entry
above: "remove the not null constraint here. The status is controlled
by the application. DB is just a dumb bag of bytes." Same underlying
philosophy as the CHECK-constraint drop, taken one step further: not
only should the DB not validate which status values are legal, it
shouldn't decide the initial one either.
Decision: applications.status in 00004's Up block changed from
`TEXT NOT NULL DEFAULT 'application_started'` to bare `TEXT` -- no
NOT NULL, no DEFAULT. notes is untouched (still `NOT NULL DEFAULT ''`)
-- the review comment was specifically about status, and notes
genuinely has no meaningful "the application decides" story the way
status's initial value does (empty notes is just... empty notes).
Ripple effects worked through in full:
- db/queries/applications.sql's CreateApplication now takes status as
  an explicit query parameter (`INSERT INTO applications (posting_id,
  status) VALUES (?, ?)`) instead of an implicit column default.
  store.CreateApplication supplies ApplicationStatusStarted.String()
  from Go -- deliberately not a hardcoded string literal moved from
  the DDL into the query SQL, which would have missed the point (Go
  decides the value, not "the DB decides it in one fewer place now").
- sqlc regeneration (nullable column): db.Application.Status,
  CreateApplicationParams.Status, and UpdateApplicationStatusParams.
  Status all became sql.NullString. store's applicationFromRow (the
  sqlc-row -> domain-struct boundary, already doing status parsing
  since the enum work above) now also checks row.Status.Valid,
  failing loudly with the row id if an actual NULL is ever
  encountered -- shouldn't happen via this package's own writes
  (CreateApplication and UpdateApplicationStatus both always supply a
  concrete value), but the column permits it, so something writing
  outside this package's API could produce one.
- Verified the explicit Valid check is technically redundant with
  ParseApplicationStatus already rejecting "" (NullString's zero
  value) on its own -- confirmed by temporarily deleting the check and
  watching TestGetApplication_NullStatusInDB_FailsLoudly still pass.
  Kept the explicit check anyway: same failure guarantee, but a
  clearer error message that names the row id, which is worth one
  extra line for a case that should never legitimately occur.
- Down migration (rebuilds applications_old with the original 00002
  NOT NULL DEFAULT + CHECK shape) needed no code change, but its
  round-trip behavior needed re-verifying since the Up side changed
  under it: manually confirmed (a) normal data (status always
  populated, since every real write goes through store) still rebuilds
  cleanly on Down, and (b) a row with an actual NULL status now causes
  Down to fail loudly with a NOT NULL constraint violation rather than
  silently coercing it to the restored default -- both consistent with
  this project's no-silent-fallback convention.
- Added TestApplicationStatusHasNoDBDefault (db/migrations/
  migrations_test.go): inserting a row without status must leave it
  NULL, not auto-populate 'application_started'.
Re-ran the same manual tophat harness used for both prior rounds of
this PR's work; confirmed byte-identical output again -- another pure
internal change with no user-visible behavior difference.

## 2026-08-19 -- Application documents (#5): filesystem, not DB blobs; presence-only in the TUI
Context: #5 originally proposed storing cover-letter/resume markdown as
two nullable TEXT columns on `applications`. A design discussion before
implementation started reversed that: the repo owner wants an external
agent/editor to consume this content directly, which a DB blob makes
awkward (no diffing, no editing in a normal editor, no easy handoff to
another tool). Superseded design, confirmed before implementation:
content lives on the filesystem; `applications` gets no new columns.

Decision: a new top-level `documents` package (mirroring `db/migrations`
as a small, dependency-free top-level package) computes convention-
derived paths from an applicationID alone --
`<base>/<applicationID>/cover_letter.md` and `.../resume.md` -- and
reports whether each exists via `os.Stat`. No path is ever persisted in
the DB; it's recomputed on every lookup. `base` is a configurable root,
threaded through as `SWAMP_DOCUMENTS_PATH` (default `"documents"`),
mirroring the existing `SWAMP_DB_PATH` pattern in `cmd/swamp/main.go`
exactly -- same shape of decision as the filename-convention conclusion
already reached for #6's capabilities document (PROFILE_REFERENCE.md):
prefer a filesystem convention over a DB-tracked path/config row when
the content itself is meant to be edited directly, not through the app.

TUI scope is deliberately presence/status only: the posting detail
screen shows "Cover Letter: found (<path>)" / "not found (<path>)" (and
the same for Resume) when an Application exists, nothing more. No
in-app viewer or editor was built -- matches #6's conclusion that these
documents are "managed by the user/agent directly on the filesystem",
not through Swamp's UI. Loading follows the existing async
tea.Cmd/tea.Msg pattern used throughout tui/app.go (see
loadApplicationDocuments/applicationDocumentsLoadedMsg) rather than a
synchronous store call inside Update, for consistency with every other
store-backed read in this file.

When no Application row exists yet for the selected posting
(`store.GetApplication` returns `store.ErrNotFound`), the documents
section is omitted entirely -- no auto-created Application, no "no
application" placeholder. Rejected auto-creating an Application on
first view: that would make merely looking at a posting have a silent
DB side effect, which is surprising and hard to reason about (a read
shouldn't write). Application creation is explicitly issue #4's job
("wire up application lifecycle in the TUI"), not shipped yet -- so
today, every posting's detail view omits the documents section
regardless of on-disk files, until #4 lands. Accepted and intentional,
not a bug: the alternative (partially building application-creation UI
inside #5 to make the section appear) would have scope-crept #5 into
#4's territory.

Verified manually end-to-end (not just unit tests): built the real
binary, applied migrations to a scratch sqlite db, inserted a company/
posting/posting_markup/application row directly via sqlite3, wrote one
of the two document files to disk, and drove the actual TUI
interactively via tmux -- confirmed the documents section is absent for
a posting with no application row, and shows correct found/not-found
status with resolved paths for a posting that has one.

## 2026-08-19 -- Application documents (#5), reworked on top of #4: reuse applicationsByPosting instead of a second fetch
Context: exactly the collision the #4 entry above flagged ("Noted
overlap risk... likely to collide with applicationsByPosting/
loadApplication here at PR-review/rebase time"). #4 merged into main
first, adding App.applicationsByPosting (populated by an async
loadApplication command fired on entering posting detail and on h/l
navigation) and changing postingDetailContent's signature to
postingDetailContent(p store.Posting, application store.Application,
hasApplication bool). This branch's original implementation (entry
above) independently fetched the same store.Application via its own
loadApplicationDocuments command/applicationDocumentsLoadedMsg, purely
to read application.ID for documents.ForApplication -- a second async
round-trip duplicating data #4's loadApplication already fetches at
the same trigger points.
Decision: merged main into this branch (git merge, not rebase --
tui/app.go changed shape enough on both sides, in the same functions,
that replaying this branch's commits one at a time via rebase would
have meant resolving the same conflict twice for no benefit, since the
loadApplicationDocuments machinery was being deleted immediately
after anyway; a single merge commit plus clean follow-up commits was
more honest about what actually happened than rewriting this branch's
history to look like it was always built against #4's API). Deleted
loadApplicationDocuments, applicationDocumentsLoadedMsg, and the
App.applicationDocuments cache entirely, along with the two
`return a, loadApplicationDocuments(...)` call sites on Enter/h/l --
all now redundant. Document existence is instead computed inline
inside postingDetailContent (given documentsBase as an added explicit
parameter, same pure-function shape #4 already established) using the
application/hasApplication values #4's loadApplication already
supplies -- no new tea.Cmd, no new App field for caching status.
Why synchronous os.Stat calls at render time are fine here despite the
rest of this file's "everything through tea.Cmd" convention: that
convention exists to keep Update side-effect-free for testability
against real store/network calls, which are comparatively slow and
worth decoupling from rendering. Checking whether two local files
exist is neither -- it's the same class of operation as formatting a
string, cheap enough (and local enough) that adding a second async
round-trip just to avoid one os.Stat call in the render path would
have been over-applying the pattern rather than following its actual
intent. documents.Paths.*Exists() remains exactly as originally built
(unchanged by this rework) -- only the caller changed, from an async
Cmd to a direct call inside postingDetailContent.
No product-behavior change from the original entry above: found/
not-found/no-application semantics, path convention, SWAMP_DOCUMENTS_
PATH config, and the documents package itself are all unchanged --
this entry is purely about which existing fetch supplies
application.ID.

## 2026-08-19 -- Application documents (#5): documents.Store wraps the base path, per PR #18 review
Context: PR review on tui/app.go's `New(s, syncer, documentsBase string)`
flagged that App held a raw base-path string and postingDetailContent
called the package-level documents.ForApplication(base, id) directly --
meaning tui knew the documents path convention, unlike its relationship
to store (App holds *store.Store, never SQL/schema details). Reviewer's
call: skip the usual "three examples before abstracting" bar here and
match the store precedent now, rather than let a second caller show up
later with the same raw-string shape.
Decision: added documents.Store (NewStore(base) *Store, method
Get(applicationID) Paths) wrapping the free ForApplication function --
the free function and its existing tests are unchanged and still used
internally, so no test coverage was lost. Method named Get rather than
ForApplication per further review feedback: ForApplication on a Store
receiver implied more than a lookup; Get matches the store.Store
convention already used throughout (GetApplication, GetPosting,
GetCompany, ...). App.documentsBase string became App.documents
*documents.Store; postingDetailContent's last parameter changed from
documentsBase string to docs *documents.Store, calling
docs.Get(application.ID) instead of documents.ForApplication(
documentsBase, application.ID). cmd/swamp/main.go now constructs
documents.NewStore(documentsPath) once at startup, same lifecycle as
store.New(sqlDB). No behavior change -- verified via full test suite
(documents and tui packages both still pass unchanged assertions,
tests updated only to call app.documents.Get instead of
documents.ForApplication(app.documentsBase, ...)).

## 2026-08-19 -- Application documents (#5): move existence checks off Paths onto Store.Status, per PR #18 review
Context: further review feedback on the Store.Get rename above -- Paths
is named and shaped like a pure value type (two path strings), but its
CoverLetterExists()/ResumeExists() methods silently performed an
os.Stat syscall on every call. A method named "XExists()" on something
called "Paths" reads like a cheap property check, not I/O; the naming
hid which one it actually was. SRP concern: Paths was doing double duty
as both a data holder and an I/O-performing service.
Decision: Paths and the free ForApplication function are unchanged and
stay pure (no methods, no I/O) -- still directly tested. Added Doc{Path
string; Exists bool} and Status{CoverLetter, Resume Doc}. Store.Get was
replaced with Store.Status(applicationID) Status, which computes Paths
internally via ForApplication and does both os.Stat calls itself,
returning path+existence bundled per document. This makes Store the
sole owner of filesystem I/O in this package -- Paths never touches the
filesystem -- and collapses postingDetailContent's two Store calls
(one implicit via paths.CoverLetterExists()/paths.ResumeExists(), one
to get the paths) into a single docs.Status(application.ID) call,
matching the design goal from the Get rename (hide the path convention
entirely behind one method call). No behavior change -- verified via
full test suite; documents_test.go's exists-behavior tests now
construct a Store and call Status instead of calling the removed
Paths methods directly, and tui/app_test.go's two document-status
tests call app.documents.Status instead of app.documents.Get.

## 2026-08-24 -- tui.App decomposition PR 1/6: extract companyListModel (RFC #19)
Context: first implementation PR against RFC #19 (parent-mediated nested
TEA models for tui.App). Scope: only the company-list screen -- lowest
risk, no dependents, proves the pattern before the harder posting-list/
detail pair (PR 6).

Decision: new tui/company_list.go defines companyListModel{store,
syncer, cursor} -- cursor is private, moved off App entirely (was
App.cursor). companies []store.Company stays App-owned domain data,
loaded via the existing companiesLoadedMsg/companyCreatedMsg/
companyDeletedMsg handlers, passed into companyListModel.Update/View by
value on every call. Two new message types (enterCompanyFormMsg,
selectCompanyMsg) represent this screen's only two cross-screen effects.

One correction made during implementation, not anticipated in the RFC:
routing screen transitions purely through tea.Cmd-produced messages (as
originally sketched) would have added an extra async round-trip that
tui/app_test.go's openPostingList helper (used by the majority of the
test suite) doesn't account for -- it runs exactly one cmd-and-feedback
cycle per keypress. Fix: companyListModel.Update returns (tea.Cmd,
tea.Msg), not just tea.Cmd -- the tea.Msg (if non-nil) is an intent
App.Update applies synchronously, in the same call, exactly matching
today's timing (screen changes and any resulting loadPostings dispatch
happen in one Update call, same as before this type existed). This is
closer to the "transition" pattern from one of the RFC's rejected
alternative designs than to pure Design-A-style Cmd-only signaling --
worth knowing since the RFC's mechanism description undersold this
timing constraint. Genuine async commands (deleteCompany, refreshCompany)
are unaffected -- returned as the first value, run through bubbletea
normally.

App.companyDeletedMsg's cursor-clamp (previously 2 inline lines) is now
a.companyList.clampCursor(len(a.companies)) -- the exact "new wrinkle"
the RFC named in advance (App can no longer reach the screen's private
cursor directly).

Verified: full existing test suite (62 tui tests) passes unchanged
after updating the ~12 call sites that read app.cursor to
app.companyList.cursor -- no test assertions or behavior changed, only
the field's location. New tui/company_list_test.go adds isolated
coverage of companyListModel directly (cursor movement/clamp, Enter/'a'
returning the right intent, no-companies edge case) with no App/store
setup required, demonstrating the RFC's stated test-isolation goal.
Manually verified against the real binary via tmux: cursor movement,
enter-to-postings, esc/b back, 'a' to form, 'd' delete with correct
cursor clamp (2 companies -> delete first -> cursor correctly lands on
the remaining one), 'q' quit.

## 2026-08-24 -- tui.App decomposition PR 2/6: extract companyFormModel (RFC #19)
Context: second implementation PR against RFC #19. Scope: the
add-company form -- like the company list, has zero cross-screen reads
today, so isolates cleanly.

Decision: new tui/company_form.go defines companyFormModel{store,
inputs, focus} -- App.formInputs/App.formFocus move onto it entirely
and become private. formFieldName/formFieldSourceRef/formFieldCount
constants moved into the same file for cohesion (previously declared
at App's top). The previous "reset in place" pattern on entry
(App.formInputs[i].SetValue(""); Blur(); formInputs[0].Focus(), run
from the company-list's 'a' handler) is replaced by constructing a
fresh companyFormModel via newCompanyFormModel(store) each time
enterCompanyFormMsg is handled -- same effect, construction instead of
post-hoc mutation, consistent with PR 1's pattern and the RFC's
seed-at-construction convention for the harder screens still to come.

One new message type, cancelCompanyFormMsg, represents Esc -- the only
cross-screen effect this form has (submitting via Enter dispatches
createCompany as a real async Cmd and stays on the same screen;
companyCreatedMsg's existing central handler already does the
screen-back-to-list transition once creation succeeds, unchanged).

Verified: full test suite (62 tui tests, unchanged assertions -- only
app.formInputs/app.formFocus call sites became
app.companyForm.inputs/app.companyForm.focus) plus 5 new isolated
companyFormModel tests (focus-on-construct, tab-advances-focus,
esc-returns-cancel, enter-with-empty-fields-is-noop,
enter-with-valid-fields-returns-createCompany-cmd) requiring no
App/store setup beyond a *store.Store passed straight into the
constructor (nil is fine for cases that never invoke the returned cmd).
Manually verified against the real binary via tmux: 'a' opens a blank
form, typing populates the focused field, tab moves focus and blurs
the previous field, enter with both fields filled creates the company
and returns to the list, esc cancels without creating anything, and a
fresh 'a' after a cancel starts blank again (no leaked state from the
cancelled attempt).

## 2026-08-24 -- tui.App decomposition PR 3/6: extract filterSelectModel (RFC #19)
Context: third implementation PR against RFC #19. Scope: the
department/location filter-select screen.

Decision: new tui/filter_select.go defines filterSelectModel{store,
companyID, companyName, departmentOptions, locationOptions, cursor,
selectedDepartments, selectedLocations} -- all five App fields this
screen owned (filterDepartmentOptions, filterLocationOptions,
filterCursor, filterSelectedDepartments, filterSelectedLocations) move
onto it and become private, along with the two App methods that only
this screen used (filterItemAtCursor, selectedFilterValues), now
unexported methods on the model. activeFilterDepartments/
activeFilterLocations stay App-owned domain data, unchanged -- they're
a different concept (the company's currently-applied filters, shown in
the posting list header) already distinct in the code from this
screen's in-progress editing state.

Construction differs from PR 1/2's "seed at key-press time" pattern:
filter options are loaded asynchronously (loadFilterOptions, dispatched
from screenPostingList's 'f' handler, which stays inline in App since
posting-list isn't extracted until PR 6) -- so filterSelectModel can't
be constructed synchronously the way companyFormModel is on 'a'. It's
instead constructed inside filterOptionsLoadedMsg's existing central
handler, once the async load resolves, replacing what were previously
5 separate field assignments with one newFilterSelectModel(...) call.
This is the same "construct fresh from a *LoadedMsg" shape PR 6's
postingDetailModel will need for the harder pair -- confirms the
pattern generalizes to async-seeded screens, not just key-press-seeded
ones.

cancelFilterSelectMsg represents Esc/'b' (the only cross-screen
effect); Enter dispatches saveCompanyFilters as a real async Cmd and
stays on-screen -- companyFiltersSavedMsg's existing central handler
(unchanged) does the screen-back-to-posting-list transition,
postings-narrowing, and refreshCompany dispatch once saving succeeds.

Verified: full test suite (62 tui tests, unchanged assertions -- only
app.filterDepartmentOptions/filterLocationOptions/
filterSelectedDepartments call sites became
app.filterSelect.departmentOptions/locationOptions/
selectedDepartments) plus 6 new isolated filterSelectModel tests
(seeds existing selections from saved filters, itemAtCursor spans
departments then locations correctly including out-of-range, cursor
clamps to total, space toggles, esc returns cancel, enter returns
save cmd). Manually verified against the real binary via tmux:
opening filters loads both departments and locations, cursor moves
correctly through the combined department-then-location list, space
toggles a checkbox, enter saves and narrows the posting list (header
shows "Filtering: Department: Engineering | Location: Remote"),
re-opening filter-select after a save correctly re-seeds both
selections as checked, and esc cancels back to the posting list
without altering the saved state.

## 2026-08-24 -- tui.App decomposition PR 4/6: extract applicationStatusModel (RFC #19)
Context: fourth implementation PR against RFC #19. Scope: the
application-status-select screen.

Decision: new tui/application_status.go defines
applicationStatusModel{store, postingID, cursor} -- App.applicationStatusCursor
becomes private cursor on the model. applicationStatuses (the fixed
status list) and applicationStatusIndex (cursor-seeding helper) moved
into the same file for cohesion -- previously declared at the top of
app.go, used nowhere else.

Seeding follows PR 2's synchronous, key-press-time pattern (not PR 3's
async one): posting-detail's 's' handler already computes
applicationStatusIndex(app.Status) inline before this PR, so
newApplicationStatusModel(store, postingID, currentStatus) just moves
that same computation into a constructor call, capturing postingID too
so the model is fully self-contained for both cursor movement and the
eventual save -- posting-detail's 's' handler stays inline in App for
now (posting-list/detail aren't extracted until PR 6), but no longer
needs to reach into App state on behalf of this screen once it's
entered. applicationStatusUpdatedMsg's existing central handler
(unchanged) does the save-then-return-to-detail transition once
updateApplicationStatus resolves -- this screen never sees that
message itself.

cancelApplicationStatusMsg represents Esc/'b' -- the only cross-screen
effect. No test file changes were needed this time: no existing test
asserted on app.applicationStatusCursor by name.

Verified: full test suite (62 tui tests, fully unchanged -- first PR
in this series requiring zero app_test.go edits) plus 4 new isolated
applicationStatusModel tests (cursor seeded from current status on
construction, cursor movement clamps to the fixed status count in both
directions, esc returns cancel, enter returns the update cmd). Manually
verified against the real binary via tmux: pressing 's' on a posting
with an application opens the screen with the cursor already on the
current status, j/k moves the cursor, enter saves the newly selected
status and returns to detail with the updated status visible (and
persisted in the DB), esc cancels without persisting anything.

## 2026-08-24 -- tui.App decomposition PR 5/6: extract applicationNotesModel (RFC #19)
Context: fifth implementation PR against RFC #19. Scope: the
application-notes-edit screen. Same shape as PR 4 (synchronous,
key-press-time construction) since notes editing is entered from
posting-detail's 'n' handler exactly like status-select's 's'.

Decision: new tui/application_notes.go defines
applicationNotesModel{store, postingID, textarea} --
App.notesTextarea becomes private, paired with the postingID it's
editing (previously read from a.postings[a.postingCursor] at both
construction and save time; now captured once at construction).
newApplicationNotesModel(store, postingID, notes, width, height)
replaces the previous 5-line construct-and-configure block
(textarea.New/SetWidth/SetHeight/SetValue/Focus) inline in the 'n'
handler. cancelApplicationNotesMsg represents Esc -- the only
cross-screen effect; ctrl+s dispatches updateApplicationNotes as a
real async Cmd and stays on-screen, same as PR 4's Enter-to-save.
applicationNotesUpdatedMsg's existing central handler (unchanged)
does the save-then-return-to-detail transition.

Verified: full test suite (62 tui tests -- one call site,
app.notesTextarea.Value(), became app.applicationNotes.textarea.Value())
plus 4 new isolated applicationNotesModel tests (seeds textarea value
and focus on construction, esc returns cancel, ctrl+s returns save
cmd, typing a key updates the textarea's value through its own
Update). Manually verified against the real binary via tmux: 'n' on a
posting with an application opens the screen with existing notes
pre-filled and focused, typing appends text, ctrl+s saves and returns
to detail with the updated notes visible (and persisted in the DB),
and a second 'n' + edit + esc discards the edit without touching the
