## 2026-08-24 -- tui.App decomposition PR 6/6 (final): extract postingListModel + postingDetailModel (RFC #19)
Context: sixth and final implementation PR against RFC #19 -- the hard
pair the RFC's design work centered on, reaching the RFC's declared end
state. Scope: the posting-list and posting-detail screens.

Decision, postingListModel{store, cursor}: same shape as PR 1's
companyListModel. postings/postingMarkup/hideArchived/activeFilter*
stay App-owned domain data, passed in as a postingListSnapshot on every
call. Four new intent messages (backToCompanyListMsg,
enterPostingDetailMsg, enterFilterSelectMsg, toggleHideArchivedMsg)
cover this screen's cross-screen effects; i/x/o dispatch real async
commands directly via the model's own store reference, same as PR 1's
company list.

Decision, postingDetailModel{store, documents, viewport, posting,
application, hasApplication}: the RFC's harder design, implemented
close to as specified but with one deliberate change made during
implementation -- applicationsByPosting stays App-owned (not moved
into this model), because it's a genuine cross-visit cache the async
loadApplication/applicationLoaded round trip depends on; removing it
would have meant every re-visit to a previously-viewed posting
flashing "no application" until the async refresh resolved. Instead,
App.lookupPosting(id) seeds a fresh postingDetailModel from the map at
every construction point (entering from the list, h/l navigation, and
the four application-mutating *UpdatedMsg handlers reconstructing the
currently-displayed posting after a save) -- postingDetailModel itself
never holds a reference to the map, matching the RFC's "snapshot, not
shared pointer" principle even though the map's contents feed it.
newPostingDetailModel always rebuilds the viewport fresh (matching the
pre-extraction showPostingDetail's "always rebuild, always reset
scroll" behavior) rather than mutating an existing instance in place.

Two real design gaps surfaced only once this was wired into App and
tested against real key sequences, not caught by isolated unit tests
alone:

1. h/l navigation inside detail no longer touches postingList.cursor
   (the two models are genuinely independent, per the isolation goal)
   -- but returning to the list via esc/b used to implicitly preserve
   cursor position for free, since postingCursor was one shared field.
   Fix: postingList.setCursor(i) plus an explicit reconciliation step
   in App's backToPostingListMsg handling
   (a.postingList.setCursor(indexOfPosting(a.postings,
   a.postingDetail.posting.ID))) -- the parent syncs the two models at
   exactly the one transition boundary where it's user-visible, rather
   than either model needing to know about the other. Caught by two
   existing app_test.go tests
   (TestApp_PostingDetail_RightMovesToNextPostingStayingInDetail,
   TestApp_PostingDetail_VimHL_MovesBetweenPostingsLikeArrows) that
   asserted directly on the old shared postingCursor field -- fixing
   them to assert on app.postingDetail.posting.Title instead (the
   actually-observable behavior) surfaced that assumption was baked
   into the old field, not derivable from user-visible behavior alone.

2. Those same two tests' fixture data had backwards title expectations
   once written to check actual posting correspondence rather than raw
   cursor arithmetic: ListPostingsByCompany orders first_seen_at DESC,
   id DESC, so with two postings sharing a first_seen_at (one sync
   batch), the later-inserted one (higher id) sorts first -- the
   original tests never caught this because they only asserted the
   cursor int incremented, never which posting was actually at that
   index. Not a bug this PR introduced; a pre-existing ordering fact
   the old tests happened not to depend on.

resetCursorIfOutOfBounds (distinct from PR 1/3's clampCursor, which
lands on the last valid index) preserves companyFiltersSavedMsg's
original "reset to 0" semantics after a filter re-narrows the list --
named and kept separate rather than reusing clampCursor for a
different behavior at that call site.

Verified: full test suite (62 tui tests -- ~25 postingCursor and ~15
detailViewport call sites renamed to postingList.cursor/
postingDetail.viewport; two tests reworked as described above; no
other behavior changes) plus 17 new isolated tests across
posting_list_test.go and posting_detail_test.go (cursor
movement/clamp/reset, all five intent messages, self-contained i/o
commands, application-guarded a/s/n handling, resize). Manually
verified against the real binary via tmux with 3 seeded postings:
list cursor movement, i/x toggles with immediate archive-hide removal
and A-toggle restore, enter/esc round-trip with the new cursor-sync
fix confirmed working (esc from detail after h/l navigation correctly
lands the list cursor on the last-viewed posting, not a stale one),
h/l navigation across all 3 postings with correct content at each
step, a/s/n sub-screens (status cursor correctly seeded, notes
correctly seeded, both save-and-return-to-detail correctly), f
entering filter-select from the list, window resize while on detail,
and q quit. One finding, confirmed pre-existing on main (not a
regression from this PR, left out of scope): the posting-detail
title line doesn't render in this tmux/terminal setup -- reproduced
identically against a from-main build with the same binary/db/key
sequence before any of PR 1-6's changes existed, so it predates this
entire decomposition effort.

This is the final PR in the RFC #19 series -- App now holds no
per-screen UI-only fields beyond the 7 screen model values themselves;
all UI-only state (cursors, form inputs, textareas, viewports) is
private to its owning screen, and all cross-screen effects flow
through typed messages the parent interprets, closing the
encapsulation gap the RFC set out to close.

## 2026-08-26 -- Agent hand-off mechanism: stage package (#26)
Context: the piece the project's been building toward since the 2026-08-10
v1-scope decision ("resume generation needs good data first") and the
documents (#5) / capabilities-document (#6) work -- a CLI mechanism an
external agent can use to discover interested postings and get everything
it needs to draft a tailored cover letter and resume. Swamp itself never
drafts content or calls an LLM; that stays entirely external, consistent
with #5's "consumed directly by an external agent" framing for the
documents package.

Decision, new `stage` package, not `handoff`: the original PRD draft named
this package `handoff`, describing the interaction between Swamp and the
external agent rather than the responsibility the package owns -- flagged
in PR review as naming smell (meta/architectural language, not domain
vocabulary, unlike `sync`/`documents`/`store`). Renamed to `stage`: the
package stages an application for drafting -- gets it into a
ready-and-waiting state without doing the drafting itself.

Decision, single constructed type mirroring sync.Syncer: `stage.New(store,
documents)` returns one `*Stage` value with `List`/`Prepare` methods, not
loose package-level functions -- same shape as `sync.New(...)` -> `*Syncer`
-> `.SyncAll(...)`. Neither `store` nor `documents` import each other or
gain any new dependency; `stage` is the only package that knows about
both, exactly how `sync` is the only package that knows about both `ashby`
and `store`.

Decision, List (read-only) vs Prepare (mutating) split: continues the
project's existing "no silent DB side effects from a read" principle (see
#5's design discussion). `List` queries interested+non-archived postings
via a new store query (postings JOIN posting_markup JOIN companies LEFT
JOIN applications) and filters out any whose application already has both
documents on disk (checked via `documents.Store.Status`) -- safe to call
as often as needed. `Prepare` is the only mutating entry point: gets or
creates the posting's Application (idempotent, mirrors the TUI's existing
"no-op if already exists" behavior for the `a` key), then calls a new
`documents.Store.EnsureDir` to create the document directory if needed.
`documents.Store.Status` and `ForApplication` stay unchanged and
read-only; `EnsureDir` is additive, not a change to that contract.

Decision, CLI surface: two subcommands added to the existing bare
`os.Args` switch in cmd/swamp/main.go, same pattern as `fetch` (no CLI
framework) -- `stage list` prints List's result as a JSON array, `stage
prepare <posting-id>` prints Prepare's result as a JSON object.

Finding during manual verification: `store.ApplicationStatus` (a Go
int-enum) serialized to JSON as a bare integer (`0`) rather than its DB
string form ("application_started") -- undermines the entire point of
machine-readable output, since an external agent would need to know
Swamp's internal enum ordering. Fixed by adding
`ApplicationStatus.MarshalJSON` (delegates to the existing `String()`),
covered by a new test in application_status_test.go. Caught by actually
running the built binary against a scratch db and reading the JSON output,
not by the unit tests alone -- worth noting as a case where the
"documents"-style manual-verification step earned its keep.

No new DB tables/columns beyond the one new SELECT query. No LLM client,
API key handling, or provider code added anywhere in Swamp. TUI unchanged
-- the existing found/not-found document display already reflects this
mechanism's output with no modification needed. PROFILE_REFERENCE.md
untouched, per #6's existing resolution.

Verified: `go build ./...`, `go test ./...` (all packages green, including
11 new stage package tests and 2 new documents/application_status tests),
`golangci-lint run ./...` (0 issues), `gofmt` check clean. Manually
verified the built binary end-to-end against a scratch sqlite db and
documents directory: list excludes a posting with no posting_markup row
and one not marked interested; prepare creates the application and
directory and is idempotent on a second call (second call's Exists flags
correctly reflect a file written between calls); list excludes a posting
once both documents exist on disk; ApplicationStatus renders as a string
after the MarshalJSON fix.

## 2026-08-30 -- Rename documents package/convention to assets
Context: the on-disk storage location for an application's cover
letter/resume markdown was named "documents" throughout (Go package,
SWAMP_DOCUMENTS_PATH env var, default base directory) since #5. Renamed
to "assets" on request.

Decision: full rename, not just the default folder name -- Go package
`documents` -> `assets` (directory and files renamed to match: assets.go,
assets_test.go), `SWAMP_DOCUMENTS_PATH` -> `SWAMP_ASSETS_PATH`, default
base directory `"documents"` -> `"assets"`. Every call site across
cmd/swamp/main.go, stage, and tui updated (import paths, struct field
names App.assets/postingDetailModel.assets, constructor params). Left a
package named `documents` pointing at an `assets` folder would have been
the exact class of naming inconsistency flagged and fixed once already
this project (`handoff` -> `stage`, see the 2026-08-26 entry) -- not
worth reintroducing here.

Not renamed: the TUI's user-facing "Documents" section label and the
plain English word "documents" used generically in comments/tests to
mean "the cover letter and resume" -- those describe the artifacts
themselves, not the storage mechanism, and weren't part of what was
asked.

The package's exported API (Store, Paths, Doc, Status, ForApplication,
NewStore, EnsureDir) needed no changes -- none of those identifiers
named "documents" in the first place, only the package/env var/folder
name did.

Existing real on-disk content (an application's already-drafted
cover_letter.md/resume.md under the old documents/ directory) was moved
to assets/ as part of this change, not left orphaned.

Verified: `go build ./...`, `go test ./...` (all packages green,
including the renamed `assets` package), `golangci-lint run ./...` (0
issues), `gofmt` check clean. Manually re-ran `swamp stage list` and
`swamp stage prepare` against the real project database via `direnv exec
.` after re-approving the changed .envrc -- confirmed existing
application content survived the directory move and new paths resolve
under assets/ with Exists correctly reported.

## 2026-08-30 -- Correction: revert the documents->assets Go rename, keep only the storage path renamed
Context: the previous entry (same date, "Rename documents package/convention
to assets") over-scoped the request. The actual ask was narrower: only the
on-disk storage path for an application's documents should be named
"assets" -- not the Go package, not SWAMP_DOCUMENTS_PATH, not any struct
field. Reverted all of that: package is `documents` again (directory and
files back to documents/documents.go, documents/documents_test.go), env
var is `SWAMP_DOCUMENTS_PATH` again, struct fields are
App.documents/postingDetailModel.documents/Stage.documents again.

What's actually kept from the previous entry: the default value of
SWAMP_DOCUMENTS_PATH changed from "documents" to "assets"
(cmd/swamp/main.go), and the real on-disk content that was already moved
to assets/<id>/ stays there (that's the correct location now, unrelated
to what the Go package is named).

Lesson: "renamed throughout" in an AskUserQuestion option description is
not the same as confirmed scope -- when a rename touches identifiers
beyond what was literally asked (a filesystem path vs. a Go package/env
var/struct field are different things even if they share a word), that's
worth a separate, explicit confirmation rather than inferring "throughout"
covers all of them from one recommended option.

Verified: `go build ./...`, `go test ./...` (all packages green, package
name back to `documents`), `golangci-lint run ./...` (0 issues), `gofmt`
check clean. Manually re-ran `swamp stage prepare` against the real
project database -- confirmed paths still resolve correctly under
assets/<id>/ with existing content intact.

## 2026-08-30 -- Filter select screen scrolls with cursor on long lists (#14)
Context: filterSelectModel.View() (tui/filter_select.go) rendered its
entire department+location option list unconditionally, with no
windowing against terminal height -- unlike company_list and posting_list,
which both already bound rendering to visible rows via visibleWindow(cursor,
n, listRows). For a company with a long department/location list (e.g. a
real company in the dev db has 18 departments + 28 locations), moving the
cursor past what fit on screen left it off-screen instead of scrolling the
view to follow it.

Decision: extract filterWindow(cursor, deptCount, locCount, rows) as a new
pure, unit-tested helper (tui/filter_select.go, tests in
filter_select_test.go) rather than inlining the windowing math into View().
It wraps the existing visibleWindow over the combined department+location
index space, then splits the resulting [start,end) back into per-group
[start,end) ranges. rows is a budget for option lines only -- a group's
section header ("Department"/"Location") renders "for free" whenever that
group has at least one option in the visible window, and disappears
entirely once the window scrolls past it, so it's never shown orphaned
with no items under it. Matches this project's existing pattern of keeping
windowing math in small tested functions (visibleWindow itself) separate
from View(), which this project's testing decisions explicitly exclude
from automated coverage (see tui package doc comment).

filterSelectModel.View gained a listRows int parameter, matching
company_list/posting_list's existing View(items, listRows) shape; the
call site in App.View passes a.listRows(), same as the other two list
screens.

Verified: go build/test/lint/fmt all clean, including 6 new filterWindow
table-driven test cases (fits everything, zero rows, cursor deep in
locations hiding the department header, cursor in departments hiding the
location header, window straddling both groups, zero departments).
Manually verified against the real project database via tmux (15-row
terminal, cohere's 18 departments + 28 locations): windowed rendering
matches the row budget on first entry, scrolling down through the full
list keeps the cursor visible throughout, the straddle case correctly
shows both headers with partial groups when the window spans the
boundary, the Department header disappears once fully scrolled past, and
both ends clamp correctly (first/last item stays visible without
overscrolling).

## 2026-08-30 -- Greenhouse job board support: client + sync multi-source routing (#30)
Context: step 1 of adding Greenhouse support (issue #30) -- a client
package plus the sync.PostingFetcher decoupling its own doc comment had
been waiting on since the 2026-08-11 decision ("deliberately not built
ahead of having a second real example to design it against"). Step 2 (TUI
add-company source picker, createCompany hardcoding "ashby") is
explicitly out of scope for this pass -- sequenced separately per Bob's
own breakdown of the work.

Decision, verify against real docs and real live data before designing:
fetched docs.greenhouse.io directly rather than working from memory, then
probed several real companies' live Greenhouse boards (gitlab, discord,
airbnb) rather than trusting the docs alone. This caught a real bug the
docs didn't mention: Greenhouse's `content` field is HTML-entity-*double*-
encoded -- its JSON string value is itself entity-escaped markup (e.g.
"&lt;p&gt;" not "<p>"). Missing this would have meant DescriptionHTML
wasn't actually renderable HTML and DescriptionText showed raw tag syntax
as visible text instead of stripped plain text. Caught by updating the
test fixture to match the real encoding (turning a passing-by-coincidence
test into a real red), not by the live-data check alone -- the fixture
now guards against regressing this specifically.

Decision, three real design points where Greenhouse's shape doesn't map
cleanly onto Ashby's (confirmed against docs.greenhouse.io/job-board.html,
not guessed): (1) a Greenhouse job can belong to multiple departments
(array), joined into one "; "-separated string since store/filter expect
a single value per posting -- Ashby postings are single-department today
so this doesn't change existing behavior; (2) DescriptionText is derived
via a real HTML tokenizer (golang.org/x/net/html, already in the module
graph as an indirect dep, promoted to direct rather than adding a new
external dependency) rather than left empty, since it's what stage.List
surfaces to an external agent for drafting -- a naive regex stripper was
rejected because it gets entity decoding and <script>/<style> exclusion
wrong; (3) EmploymentType/WorkplaceType are left empty for Greenhouse
postings -- Greenhouse's job board API doesn't expose them in a
structured, cross-company-reliable way (only via per-company-configurable
metadata).

Decision, sync.Posting + per-source adapters over a shared interface on
the client packages: sync gets its own source-agnostic Posting type
(identical shape to what ashby.Posting already was). Each source package
(ashby, greenhouse) keeps its own native Posting type matching what that
source's API actually returns -- greenhouse.Posting has no Team/
EmploymentType/WorkplaceType fields at all, rather than padding it with
fields that source can never populate. AshbyFetcher/GreenhouseFetcher are
thin adapters translating each into sync.Posting and satisfying
PostingFetcher. Syncer holds map[string]PostingFetcher keyed by
store.Company.Source instead of one fetcher; SyncCompany looks up the
configured source and errors on an unconfigured one, same failure mode as
the old ashby-only check. Adding a third source (e.g. #31's Lever) is one
more map entry in cmd/swamp/main.go's newSyncer, no interface changes.

Verified: go build/test/lint/fmt all clean across the whole repo,
including greenhouse's tests (client + stripHTML, both against
fixtures) and sync's tests (existing SyncCompany/SyncAll suite migrated
to the new Posting type and map-based New, plus new AshbyFetcher/
GreenhouseFetcher translation tests). Manually verified against gitlab's
real, live Greenhouse board through the actual client (not just the
fixture): 220 real postings, description HTML/text correctly decoded and
stripped. Also manually verified the full sync pipeline end to end
through the built CLI binary against a scratch db: inserted a
source="greenhouse" company pointed at gitlab's real board, ran `swamp
fetch`, confirmed 220 postings created with sane title/department/
location, and confirmed a second fetch was idempotent (0 created).

## 2026-08-30 -- Company form source picker: step 2 of Greenhouse support (#30)
Context: step 2 of #30 -- letting a user actually add a Greenhouse company
through the TUI, not just via direct SQL. Branched off greenhouse-job-
board-support's tip (not main) since it depends on that PR's sync/
createCompany plumbing.

Decision: source selection is a third field in the existing add-company
form (Tab-cycled alongside Name/SourceRef), not a separate screen.
Picking a source and filling in the board slug/token felt like one
coherent action, not two -- a dedicated screen-then-screen flow would add
a navigation round-trip for what's fundamentally still "fill out one
form." companySources ([]string{"ashby", "greenhouse"}) and
sourceRefLabels (which label the second field gets, since "Ashby slug"
and "Greenhouse board token" mean different things) live in
company_form.go itself, not derived from sync's fetcher map -- there's no
existing canonical exported list of supported sources to derive from
without inventing new cross-package plumbing for two hardcoded strings;
a comment flags that this list must be kept in sync with
cmd/swamp/main.go's newSyncer by hand.

Decision, form layout: kept `inputs []textinput.Model` sized
formFieldCount (3) with formFieldSource's slot simply unused as a
textinput, rather than restructuring around 2 named fields -- this let
the existing formFieldName/formFieldSourceRef constants and every
existing test assertion that references them by name keep working
unchanged (only their integer values shifted, invisibly, since nothing
hardcoded the literal 0/1). Source cycles via left/right arrow only when
it's the focused field -- guarded explicitly so arrow keys don't leak
into the source picker while a text field is focused (a textinput
consumes arrow keys itself, for cursor movement within the field).

Focus starts on the source field (not Name, as it did before) --
picking/confirming a source is now the natural first step of the flow,
and the picker's current selection is always visible in View() regardless
of focus, so nothing is hidden by starting there.

createCompany gained a source parameter (previously hardcoded "ashby");
its one call site in companyFormModel.Update now passes
companySources[m.sourceIndex].

Verified: go build/test/lint/fmt all clean, including 8 new
company_form tests (source-field-first focus, 3-field tab cycling,
left/right cycling on and off the source field, and an end-to-end test
using a real store confirming the selected source actually lands in the
created company row) and 3 existing App-level integration tests updated
for the new field order (they previously assumed focus started on Name).
Manually verified against the real built binary via tmux: opened the
form, confirmed the source picker renders with ashby boxed by default
and the second field labeled "Ashby slug", toggled to greenhouse and
confirmed the label switched to "Greenhouse board token", submitted a
real Greenhouse company (GitLab, board token "gitlab") and confirmed it
stored with source=greenhouse, then pressed 'r' to refresh and confirmed
it synced for real against GitLab's live Greenhouse board (220 postings
created) -- proving the full chain (TUI form -> store -> sync routing ->
GreenhouseFetcher -> real API) works end to end together. Also verified
submitting with the default (ashby) source unchanged, no regression.

## 2026-08-30 -- Edit an existing company's name (#35)
Context: #35, narrowed during scoping discussion to name-only editing --
source/source_ref are locked at creation and have no edit path at all,
since changing either really means "this is a different board," not
"edit this company," and already-ingested postings.source/source_id
values wouldn't retroactively reflect a change anyway (see the issue's
"Out of scope" section, and #36 for the separate create-time validation
idea that came out of the same discussion).

Decision: a new, separate companyEditModel (tui/company_edit.go) rather
than adding an edit mode to companyFormModel. Mirrors applicationNotesModel's
shape (store + target ID + single focused input, esc cancels, a save key
dispatches a command) more closely than companyFormModel's multi-field
Tab-cycling shape -- editing is a single field, so there's no field-cycling
or source-picker concern to inherit. store gained UpdateCompanyName
(new sqlc query, same deleted_at IS NULL guard as GetCompany -- editing a
soft-deleted company isn't supported, matching how it's already invisible
everywhere else).

New 'e' keybinding on the company list, alongside the existing a/d/r.
Wired the same way enterApplicationNotesMsg/applicationNotesUpdatedMsg
already are: an intent type (enterCompanyEditMsg) defined in
company_list.go where it's emitted, a command + result msg
(updateCompanyName/companyNameUpdatedMsg) defined in app.go alongside
createCompany/deleteCompany, and the result handler updates a.companies
in place by ID (same pattern as companyDeletedMsg, but replacing instead
of removing).

Verified: go build/test/lint/fmt all clean, including 3 new store tests
(name changes, unchanged Source/SourceRef, ErrNotFound for a nonexistent
or soft-deleted company), 5 new companyEditModel tests, 2 new
companyListModel tests for the 'e' key, and one new App-level integration
test covering the full flow (press e, confirm the field is seeded from
the selected company, edit, submit, confirm both the in-memory list and
the stored row reflect the new name while ID/Source/SourceRef stay
unchanged). Manually verified against the real built binary via tmux:
added a company, pressed e, confirmed the name field is pre-seeded,
appended text and saved, confirmed the list shows the updated name and
the DB row kept the same id/source/source_ref, and confirmed esc after
typing cancels without saving.

## 2026-08-31 -- Move project instructions to AGENTS.md, CLAUDE.md imports it
Context: the repo's instructions file (formerly CLAUDE.md) had no Claude-
specific content in it -- it was pure repo/engineering workflow guidance
(issue workflow, testing, branching, tooling, decisions log) that applies
identically to any coding agent working in this codebase. AGENTS.md is
the emerging cross-tool convention for exactly this.

Decision: AGENTS.md is now the source of truth (verbatim content, moved
wholesale, only the intro line reworded to be tool-agnostic). CLAUDE.md
is a real, small file (not a symlink) containing `@AGENTS.md` -- Claude
Code's documented import syntax -- so Claude Code's own auto-discovery
(which looks for CLAUDE.md specifically, confirmed it has no first-class
AGENTS.md support) still picks up the same content, with room to add
genuinely Claude-Code-specific instructions below the import later if any
ever arise. A symlink (`ln -s AGENTS.md CLAUDE.md`) was considered and
rejected in favor of two real files -- explicit preference, not a
technical constraint (the symlink is verified to work fine on macOS/
Linux; Windows needs Admin/Developer Mode for it, which the import
syntax sidesteps entirely, but that wasn't the deciding factor here).

Also folded in a process fix while touching this file: added an explicit
"use GitHub's closing-keyword syntax (Fixes #N), not a bare #N reference"
line to the feature-branch workflow section, from the #35-stayed-open-
after-#42-merged incident earlier the same day.

Verified: both files read back correctly, diffed AGENTS.md's body against
the prior CLAUDE.md to confirm only the two intended changes (reworded
intro line, new closing-keyword item) -- no accidental content loss in
the move.

## 2026-08-31 -- apply-to-posting skill moved to .agents/skills/, symlinked from .claude/skills/
Context: continuing the AGENTS.md migration's spirit (tool-agnostic
canonical location, not buried under a tool-specific dotdir) for the
apply-to-posting skill, which already had no Claude-specific wording in
its body.

Unlike CLAUDE.md, Claude Code has no import syntax for skills -- verified
via claude-code-guide against the real docs. Skills are only discovered
at `.claude/skills/<name>/SKILL.md`, hard-coded, no settings.json search
path option. The only supported way to keep one canonical copy outside
that dir is a symlink; Claude Code explicitly documents following
symlinked skill directories.

Directory name went through a correction: first landed on `agent/skills/`
(matching the singular, un-dotted feel of `AGENTS.md`), but Bob flagged
`.agents/skills/` as the standard he'd seen. Checked both claims before
picking: web search says `.agents/skills/` is a widely-cited convention
tied to the open Agent Skills format spec at agentskills.io; a direct
doc check via claude-code-guide found Claude Code's own docs mention only
`.claude/skills/` (personal/project), enterprise-managed, and plugin
paths -- no `.agents/skills/` anywhere. The two aren't actually in
conflict: agentskills.io standardizes the SKILL.md file format, not a
discovery path -- each tool picks its own path independently, and
Claude Code hasn't adopted `.agents/skills/` as one. So naming it
`.agents/skills/` doesn't buy native discovery either way; it was Bob's
explicit call once that was laid out, made for consistency with the
convention he's seen elsewhere.

Decision: canonical file is now `.agents/skills/apply-to-posting/
SKILL.md` (moved with `git mv` to preserve history, after first moving
through `agent/skills/`). `.claude/skills/apply-to-posting` is a symlink
to `../../.agents/skills/apply-to-posting`. This is the opposite call
from the CLAUDE.md/AGENTS.md split (real file there, symlink rejected)
-- different situation, since there's no lightweight import mechanism
for a multi-file skill the way `@AGENTS.md` covers a single small file.

Verified: symlink resolves (`ls -la .claude/skills/` shows it pointing at
`../../.agents/skills/apply-to-posting`, `readlink` confirms, `SKILL.md`
readable through it), `git status` shows a rename for the skill file
(history preserved, not add+delete), grepped AGENTS.md/README.md for
stale `.claude/skills`, `agent/skills`, or `SKILL.md` path references --
none found.

## 2026-08-31 -- Posting list columns aligned with lipgloss/table (#46)
Problem: `postingListModel.View` built each row with `fmt.Sprintf("%s %s |
%s | %s | %s", ...)` -- no fixed column widths, so the `|` separators
landed at a different horizontal position on every line. `github.com/
charmbracelet/lipgloss/table` was already sitting in the dependency graph
(a subpackage of lipgloss v1.1.0, already a direct dependency) and does
column width computation natively.

Decision: adopted `lipgloss/table` with its default bordered rendering --
Bob's comment on the issue confirmed the table doesn't need to be
borderless or replicate the old minimal look, so no custom border
stripping was needed. Columns: marker (★/✕/blank), Title, Department,
Location, Status, with a header row. Cell values are truncated to a
fixed max width per column (title 40, department 18, location 20,
status 8) with an ellipsis via `github.com/charmbracelet/x/ansi`'s
`TruncateWc` (already an indirect dependency, pulled in by lipgloss/
table itself -- promoted to direct with `go mod tidy`) before being
handed to the table, rather than relying on the table's own wrap
behavior.

This was the real decision point the issue flagged: every list screen in
this TUI (`visibleWindow` in `viewport.go`) assumes exactly one physical
line per item for its cursor/scroll math. Letting cells wrap would have
broken that assumption everywhere it's used -- truncating keeps every
row exactly one line, which is why cells are pre-truncated (width-aware,
not byte-aware, so multi-byte runes truncate correctly) rather than
handed to the table untouched and left to `lipgloss/table`'s own
wrapping.

The selected row is no longer marked with a `"> "` text prefix (awkward
inside table cells) -- instead the whole row is styled with the existing
`cursorStyle` (foreground 212) via the table's `StyleFunc`, keeping the
same visual language as the rest of the app.

The table's own border/header adds 4 physical lines beyond one per data
row (top border, header, header separator, bottom border) that aren't
accounted for in `chromeRows` (a fixed constant shared across all list
screens). Rather than touching the shared constant, `postingListModel.
View` subtracts a local `postingTableChromeLines = 4` from `listRows`
before computing `visibleWindow`'s row budget, scoped to this screen
only.

Scope: `company_list.go`'s `"%s (%s)"` rows are a label, not a table
(matches the issue's own scoping note) -- left untouched.

Verified: `TestTruncateCol` (TDD, table-driven) covers under/over/equal-
to-max and a wide-rune (CJK) case -- confirmed the expected truncated
value against `ansi.TruncateWc`'s actual output rather than assuming it,
and asserted `lipgloss.Width` of the result never exceeds max. Manually
verified against a real production dataset (913 real postings across 10
companies, copied to a scratch db) via tmux: confirmed columns align
across rows of varying length, long titles truncate with an ellipsis,
a real double-width Japanese title (`シニアコンサルティングエンジニア`)
renders and aligns correctly, cursor row highlighting still works and
coexists with the marker column, and a 41-row unfiltered table (Clickhouse)
renders within the terminal height with no overflow -- confirming the
`postingTableChromeLines` budget subtraction is correct.

## 2026-08-31 -- Four bug-labeled issues from the #35 code review (#38, #39, #41, #37)
All four were found during code review of #35 and filed as separate
issues; tackled together in one branch/PR since they're small, related,
and touch adjacent code, rather than one branch each.

**#38 -- whitespace-only company name accepted.** Both `companyFormModel`
and `companyEditModel` guarded against an empty name with `name == ""`
but never trimmed first, so `"   "` passed the check and saved a blank-
looking company. Fixed by trimming before the check in both places, and
saving the trimmed value (not just checking it) so `"  Acme  "` doesn't
persist with stray whitespace either. No downstream validation exists
(`updateCompanyName`, `store.UpdateCompanyName`, the SQL) -- the TUI
guard is the only place this can reasonably be caught.

**#39 -- company list goes out of alphabetical order after create/
rename.** `ListActiveCompanies` orders by name, but `a.companies` was
mutated in place (append on create, in-place replace on rename) with
nothing re-sorting afterward. Chose to re-sort client-side
(`sortCompaniesByName`, using Go's `<` on `Name`, which matches SQLite's
default `BINARY` collation for ASCII) rather than reloading the full list
from the store on every mutation -- smaller change, keeps the existing
synchronous update semantics the #35 test (`TestApp_PressE_
EditsCompanyName`) already depends on.

**#41 -- cancelling a screen never clears the stale error banner.**
`a.err` was only ever overwritten by the next async result, so an error
from a failed action, followed by the user cancelling out rather than
retrying, kept showing indefinitely on whatever screen they landed on
next. Fixed systemically per the issue's own suggestion, not per cancel
handler: `App.Update`'s `tea.KeyMsg` case now delegates to a new
`updateKeyMsg` method and clears `a.err` whenever that call actually
changes `a.screen` -- covers all screen transitions (cancel-out and
forward navigation alike), not just the five `cancelXMsg` handlers named
in the issue.

**#37 -- Esc doesn't cancel an in-flight company-name save.** This one
was `TBD` in the issue -- needed a decision, not just a fix. Options
laid out were: block Esc while a save is pending, queue Esc and apply it
once the save resolves, or accept the race and just fix the misleading
"without saving" wording. Bob's call, after discussion: block. The
queuing option turned out to have a real problem once worked through --
on save success the screen already auto-navigates away on its own
(nothing left for a queued Esc to do), and on save failure a queued Esc
would have to auto-navigate away too, which means silently discarding a
failed save's error instead of showing it (the "silent fallback"
anti-pattern). Blocking sidesteps both: `companyEditModel` gained a
`saving bool`, set on Enter and cleared by a new `saveResolved()` method
that `App` calls from the `companyNameUpdatedMsg` handler regardless of
success or failure. Esc is a no-op while `saving` is true; once it
clears, either the screen has already left (success) or the user is
still there with the error visible and Esc now works normally (failure).

Verified: `TestCompanyEditModel_Esc_WhileSavePending_NoOp` and
`_AfterSaveResolved_ReturnsCancelMsg` (model-level), plus
`TestApp_EscWhileCompanyNameSavePending_DoesNotRaceTheSave` (App-level,
reproduces the exact race: dispatches the save command without running
it, sends Esc, confirms the screen didn't move, then runs the command
and confirms the save landed). Confirmed each new test actually catches
the regression it targets by stashing the corresponding fix and
re-running -- #41's test fails with the stale error still present;
#37's test fails to even compile without `saveResolved` (a stronger
signal than an assertion failure). #38/#39 got the same TDD red-first
treatment inline rather than a stash check, since their fixes are single-
line and the failure mode is obvious from the assertion message.
Manually verified #38/#39 together against a real production dataset via
tmux: creating "  Zeta Corp  " saved as "Zeta Corp", inserted in the
correct alphabetical position (`BINARY` collation: uppercase before
lowercase) between the existing "Wealthsimple" and "clickhouse"; a
whitespace-only name was correctly rejected as a no-op; normal (non-
racing) edit-cancel and edit-save both still work with real save timing.

## 2026-08-31 -- AGENTS.md: one PR per issue by default, keep PRs minimally scoped
Context: PR #49 bundled four bug-labeled issues (#37, #38, #39, #41) into
one branch/PR because they were all found together during the #35 code
review and touched adjacent code. Bob flagged this after the fact: he
wants one PR per issue by default going forward, with PRs kept scoped to
the minimal change, and asked for it to be written down in AGENTS.md
rather than relied on as a one-off correction.

Decision: added an explicit rule to "Issue-driven task workflow" --
one PR per issue by default even when several issues are filed or
picked up together, don't bundle for convenience, ask first if bundling
seems to genuinely make sense for a given batch. Added a matching line
to "Feature branch workflow" generalizing the same principle beyond the
issue-batch case: don't fold in unrelated or loosely-related work just
because it's convenient to be touching the code anyway.

Note: this doesn't retroactively split #49 -- it's already open, and
splitting an already-committed, already-pushed PR after the fact would
be more churn than value. Applies going forward.

## 2026-08-31 -- Active-applications screen becomes the app's home screen (#43)
Problem: an application was only reachable by drilling into a specific
company's posting list -- no cross-company view of "everything I'm
actively applying to," no quick way to check status or open a cover
letter/resume without remembering which company/posting it belonged to.

Several real design decisions were open in the issue itself, resolved
with Bob directly before implementing:
- **Active scope**: excludes only `rejected`/`offer_declined` -- every
  other status (including `offer_received`/`offer_accepted`) still
  shows, since those can still need attention.
- **Missing document on $EDITOR open**: opens anyway. Most editors
  create the file on save; no separate "has this been prepared" gate.
- **Entry point**: not a new keybinding on the company list -- this
  screen *replaces* the company list as the app's home screen. Company
  list becomes one level deeper, reached via 'c' from here, with a new
  esc/b binding on company list to get back (it had no "back" before,
  since it was the top of the hierarchy).

Implementation:
- `store.ListActiveApplications` / `store.ActiveApplication`: an inner
  join on `applications` (unlike `ListInterestedPostings`'s left join --
  an application always exists for every row here), joined with
  `postings`/`companies` for company name and posting fields, ordered
  by `applications.updated_at DESC`. `ApplicationStatus` comes back as
  `sql.NullString` from sqlc despite the `WHERE ... NOT IN` filter
  (sqlc can't know the filter excludes NULL) -- handled the same
  defensive way `applicationFromRow` already does for `GetApplication`.
- `tui/editor.go`: `openInEditor`/`editorCommand`, a distinct mechanism
  from `openInBrowser` -- most `$EDITOR`s are terminal programs that
  need `tea.ExecProcess` (suspends the Program, hands over the TTY)
  rather than `openInBrowser`'s fire-and-forget `.Start()` for a
  detached GUI process. `editorCommand` is the pure, testable decision
  (unset `$EDITOR` -> error); the actual `os.Getenv`/`exec.Command`
  plumbing around it isn't unit-tested, mirroring `openInBrowser`'s own
  untested outer wrapper.
- `tui/active_applications.go`: new screen, table-rendered like
  `posting_list.go` (#46) for visual consistency. Keys: l/r open cover
  letter/resume in `$EDITOR` (via `documents.Store.EnsureDir`, called
  synchronously -- same "local filesystem I/O is cheap enough to not
  need a separate tea.Cmd round trip" reasoning `postingDetailContent`'s
  `docs.Status` call already established), s changes status (reuses
  `applicationStatusModel`/`enterApplicationStatusMsg` rather than a
  second status-picker), c goes to companies, q quits.
- **Return-screen tracking**: `screenApplicationStatusSelect` is now
  reachable from both `screenPostingDetail` and the new screen, but
  `applicationStatusUpdatedMsg`'s handler hardcoded "return to posting
  detail." Added `App.applicationStatusReturnScreen`, set wherever
  `enterApplicationStatusMsg` is handled, read by both the cancel path
  and the save path instead of assuming.
- **Freshness**: both `applicationCreatedMsg` (starting a new
  application from posting-detail) and `applicationStatusUpdatedMsg`
  now also return `loadActiveApplications` -- a status change can move
  an application in or out of the terminal-status filter, and a newly
  started application should appear without restarting the app. Found
  this gap while writing the App-level tests below, not in the
  original issue -- fixed rather than filed separately since it's the
  same underlying "keep the list correct" concern the screen exists for.
- `App.Init()` now returns `tea.Batch(loadCompanies, loadActiveApplications)`
  -- surfaced a real bug in the test harness: `newTestApp` previously
  ran `app.Init()()` and fed the single result straight into `Update`,
  which worked when `Init` returned exactly one `tea.Cmd`/msg pair. A
  `tea.Batch` returns a `tea.BatchMsg` (`[]tea.Cmd`) instead, which
  `App.Update` has no case for -- silently dropped, meaning companies/
  active-applications would never load in any test. Replaced with
  `applyCmd`, a small recursive helper that unpacks `BatchMsg` the way
  a real `tea.Program`'s runtime loop would, since these tests drive
  `App` directly without one.
- Home screen changing broke ~15 existing tests that assumed
  `screenCompanyList` was where `newTestApp` starts (most through the
  shared `openPostingList` helper, used by 46 tests) -- fixed at the
  helper level (`openPostingList` now presses 'c' first) plus each
  standalone test that pressed company-list keys directly, rather than
  changing `newTestApp`'s own default screen (which would have hidden
  the real behavior the new screen introduces).

Verified: full unit coverage (store join/filtering, `editorCommand`,
the new screen's keybindings, empty-list safety) plus App-level
integration tests for the return-screen fix (including a test that
would fail to compile without `saveResolved`-style tracking) and the
freshness-reload fix. Manually verified via tmux against a real
production dataset with a live `vi` as `$EDITOR`: home screen renders
real data correctly aligned; 'c'/esc round-trips between home and
company list; 's' from home correctly returns to home (not posting
detail) on both cancel and save; rejecting an application immediately
drops it from the list; starting a new application via posting-detail's
'a' key makes it appear on the home screen without a restart; 'l'/'r'
launch `vi` via `tea.ExecProcess` on the correct distinct file paths,
swamp's screen redraws cleanly after `vi` exits, and the saved content
actually persists to disk.

## 2026-08-31 -- store.ActiveApplication renamed to store.ApplicationView
Follow-up to the entry above: Bob flagged `ActiveApplication`'s name
directly -- "the adjective" (Active) doesn't earn its place, since the
struct's shape (application + posting + company name) has nothing to do
with status filtering; a rejected application joined the same way would
have the identical shape. The type doesn't represent "an active
application," it represents "an application, viewed with its posting and
company resolved" -- "active" describes what `ListActiveApplications`
selects, not what the row looks like.

Renaming straight to `Application` wasn't available -- `store.Application`
already exists (the plain `applications`-table row `GetApplication`/
`CreateApplication`/etc. return). First pass at a fix put `Posting`/
`CompanyName` directly onto `Application` itself, populated only by the
join query and zero-valued everywhere else -- Bob called this out too:
it doesn't remove the two-shapes problem, it just hides it inside one
type name instead of two, which is worse (a caller can no longer tell
from the type alone whether `.CompanyName` is real).

Landed on `ApplicationView`, embedding `Application` rather than
duplicating its fields under different names (the prior `ApplicationID`/
`ApplicationStatus` fields are gone -- `.ID`/`.Status` now come from the
embedded `Application`, promoted through). This isn't following the
`InterestedPosting` precedent (raised and explicitly set aside -- Bob's
call was to reason from the concept, not the existing pattern):
`InterestedPosting` wraps `Posting` with genuinely optional data (an
application may not exist yet, hence its `*int64`/`*ApplicationStatus`
pointer fields) -- a different concept layered on top. `ApplicationView`
is the same entity, not a different one, with context resolved that
every other caller already has in scope by the time they call
`GetApplication`. As Bob put it: in an ORM (Rails etc.) this would just
be a defined relation/include; sqlc has no such magic, so a small
hand-written view type is the direct, honest equivalent -- not a
workaround.

Query changes to support this: `ListActiveApplications` now also selects
`applications.notes`/`created_at`/`updated_at` (aliased to avoid
colliding with `postings.*`'s own columns of the same name), so the
embedded `Application` is actually complete -- not a second partial
representation that happens to reuse the real type's name.
`applicationViewFromRow` builds it via the existing `applicationFromRow`
(reused, not duplicated), passing a synthetic `db.Application` built
from the row's aliased columns.

Verified: `store` tests updated to assert on the promoted fields
directly (`v.ID`, `v.Status`, `v.PostingID`, `v.Notes` -- the last two
are new assertions this refactor made possible, since the embedded
`Application` is now provably complete rather than assumed to be), full
suite green, lint/fmt clean.

## 2026-08-31 -- ListActiveApplications switched to sqlc.embed()
Immediate follow-up to the entry above. Bob asked whether the query
pattern itself (hand-selecting+aliasing individual `postings`/
`applications` columns, then reconstructing `db.Posting`/`db.Application`
literals field-by-field in `applicationViewFromRow`) was actually the
right approach, rather than something already provided by the tooling.

Checked rather than assumed: sqlc has a native macro for exactly this,
`sqlc.embed(table)` -- it generates a nested struct field (e.g.
`Posting db.Posting`) instead of flattened columns, with the `Scan()`
call wired up automatically. Two real open questions before adopting it
here: does it work on this project's engine (sqlite) at all, and does it
coexist with a plain aliased column (`companies.name AS company_name`)
and two embeds in the same inner join? Both had documented rough edges
elsewhere (a github.com/sqlc-dev/sqlc issue describes `sqlc.embed` with
a *left* join failing on sqlite when the embedded side is NULL), so
rather than trust the docs summary, ran the exact shape against a
scratch copy of this project's real schema (`sqlc generate` against a
copy of `db/queries`+`db/migrations`, engine sqlite, sqlc v1.31.1 --
matching `go.mod`/`sqlc.yaml` exactly): `SELECT sqlc.embed(applications),
sqlc.embed(postings), companies.name AS company_name FROM ... JOIN ...`
generated exactly the target shape (`ListActiveApplicationsRow{
Application Application; Posting Posting; CompanyName string }`) with a
correct, automatic `Scan()`. The known left-join/NULL issue doesn't
apply here -- this is an inner join, application always exists.

Decision: switched `ListActiveApplications` to `sqlc.embed(applications)`
+ `sqlc.embed(postings)` + the plain `company_name` alias.
`applicationViewFromRow` now just runs `row.Application`/`row.Posting`
(already the correct nested db types) through the existing
`applicationFromRow`/`postingFromRow` conversions -- no more hand-copied
field lists that silently stop tracking the schema if a column is added
to `postings`/`applications` later. The `ApplicationView` Go type itself
(embeds `Application`, from the entry above) is unchanged -- this was
entirely a query-construction-layer improvement, not a type-shape one.

This came out of a broader conversation about idiomatic Go for
"entity + resolved related data": composition via embedding is the
Go-level idiom (settled, unrelated to sqlc); `sqlc.embed()` is the
sqlc-specific mechanism for producing that composition without hand-
copying column lists, consistent with sqlc's own query-first (not
ORM) design -- using it here isn't a stylistic preference, it's using
the tool actually built for this rather than reimplementing a piece of
it by hand.

Verified: scratch experiment against real schema+engine before touching
the real query (not assumed from docs, which were themselves incomplete/
contradictory across summarized fetches); full build+test+lint+fmt
clean after the switch; no `store`/`tui` call site needed to change,
since `ApplicationView`'s public shape didn't change -- confirms the
type-design (previous entry) and the query-construction fix (this one)
were genuinely separable decisions, not entangled.

## 2026-08-31 -- ListInterestedPostings: same sqlc.embed() pattern as ApplicationView, applied selectively
Follow-up to the `ActiveApplication` -> `ApplicationView` rename and its
`sqlc.embed()` switch (both on the unmerged `active-applications-screen`
branch, PR #53). `ListInterestedPostings` (`store/interested_posting.go`,
feeding `stage.List`) was the other query in the codebase built the same
hand-selected-columns-and-manually-reconstruct-the-struct way -- it
predates `ApplicationView` and was in fact its explicit precedent.

Applied `sqlc.embed(postings)` for the posting side: `postings` is
inner-joined via `posting_markup`, always present, exactly the case the
macro is verified safe for (see the entry above). `interestedPostingFromRow`
now runs `row.Posting` straight through `postingFromRow`, same
simplification as `applicationViewFromRow` got.

Deliberately did NOT `sqlc.embed(applications)` here. This query LEFT
JOINs `applications` (a posting can be interested with no application
started yet) -- exactly the case where the earlier research (sqlc-dev/
sqlc#2997) found `sqlc.embed` broken on sqlite: it fails scanning a NULL
embedded struct rather than reporting it as absent. Bob asked to verify
that directly rather than trust the cited issue, so it was reproduced
independently: a throwaway program against a scratch copy of this
project's real migrations (company -> posting -> posting_markup with
interested_at set -> deliberately no application row), running the
generated code for `SELECT sqlc.embed(postings), sqlc.embed(applications),
...` with the same LEFT JOIN, on this project's exact stack (sqlc
v1.31.1, sqlite via modernc.org/sqlite). Result: `sql: Scan error on
column index 21, name "id": converting NULL to int64 is unsupported` --
confirmed still broken, not fixed since the issue was filed in 2023.
Left that side as individually aliased nullable columns
(`applications.id AS application_id`, `applications.status AS
application_status`), handled by the existing `sql.NullInt64`/
`sql.NullString` `.Valid` checks -- unchanged, and correct as-is.

Also deliberately did NOT change `InterestedPosting.Posting` from a
named field to an anonymously embedded one, even though `ApplicationView`
embeds `Application`. Different situation: `ApplicationView` needed
embedding specifically to stop `ActiveApplication` from re-flattening
`Application`'s fields under new names (`ApplicationID`/`ApplicationStatus`
duplicating what `Application.ID`/`Application.Status` already say).
`InterestedPosting` never had that problem -- `Posting Posting` was
already a real, whole field holding a real `Posting` value; its
`ApplicationID *int64`/`ApplicationStatus *ApplicationStatus` are two
genuinely optional scalars, not a flattened re-encoding of `Application`
(which also has `Notes`/`CreatedAt`/`UpdatedAt` that `InterestedPosting`
doesn't need). Embedding here would be a cosmetic API-surface change
with no concrete problem behind it -- not applying "the same pattern"
just for surface-level consistency where the underlying issue it fixes
doesn't exist.

Verified: added `store/interested_posting_test.go` (no direct test
existed before -- only indirect coverage via `stage`'s tests) covering
the no-application case (fields nil), the has-application case (fields
populated), and the archived-exclusion case; ran all three against the
pre-refactor code first to confirm they're a real baseline, then again
after -- unchanged, green. `stage`'s existing test suite (the actual
consumer) also unchanged and green -- confirms the `Posting:` field-name
access pattern it uses is unaffected either way. Full build/test/lint/
fmt clean.

## 2026-09-01 -- Terminal application status: single source of truth via sqlc.slice() (#60)
Problem: `store/application_status.go` defines the canonical
`ApplicationStatus` enum, but which statuses count as "terminal"
(excluded from `ListActiveApplications`) existed only as a raw SQL
literal, `WHERE applications.status NOT IN ('rejected',
'offer_declined')` -- disconnected from the Go source of truth, with
nothing to catch drift if a status was renamed or a new terminal one
added and this line wasn't updated to match.

Considered three mechanisms, discussed with Bob:
(A) filter in Go after an unfiltered fetch -- eliminates duplication,
smallest diff, matches an existing pattern already used twice elsewhere
in this codebase (`filterOutArchived`, `narrowPostingsToFilters` in
`tui/app.go`), but loses locality of behavior: reading the SQL query
alone wouldn't show that any filtering happens at all.
(B) `sqlc.slice()` -- keep the exclusion as a real SQL `NOT IN`, but
source the actual status values from Go at call time instead of typing
them as literals in the query file.
(C) a drift-detection test asserting the SQL and Go agree, without
structurally preventing drift -- worth adding regardless of A or B.

Initially leaned A, reasoning mostly on diff size. Bob pushed back:
`sqlc.slice()` keeps Go as the sole source of truth exactly as fully as
A does (the `.sql` file ends up with zero status string literals either
way -- just a placeholder), while additionally keeping the query itself
honest about what it does, which A does not: a reader of the SQL file
alone under A would see no status filtering and have to go find the
surrounding Go code to learn any rows get dropped. That's a real
readability argument A didn't have, not just a performance one -- went
with B.

Verified before adopting, not assumed: `sqlc.slice()`'s sqlite support
has a documented ordering bug when combined with *other* bound
parameters in the same query (this query has none, but that's a
constraint on future changes to it, not a proof it's safe in general).
Ran the exact query shape against a scratch copy of this project's real
schema/stack (sqlc v1.31.1, sqlite) with real inserted data -- one
application per status, including the terminal ones -- and confirmed the
generated `ListActiveApplications(ctx, terminalStatuses
[]sql.NullString)` returns exactly the non-terminal rows before touching
the real query file.

Implementation: `store.TerminalApplicationStatuses()` (new, in
`application_status.go`) is the sole source of truth, returning a
defensive copy the same way `ApplicationStatuses()` already does.
`ListActiveApplications`'s SQL now reads `WHERE applications.status NOT
IN (sqlc.slice('terminal_statuses'))` -- no status strings in the query
file at all. `store.ListActiveApplications`'s public signature is
unchanged (`ctx` only) -- it builds the `[]sql.NullString` param from
`TerminalApplicationStatuses()` internally, so no caller outside `store`
needs to know this mechanism exists.

Also added (C) as belt-and-suspenders, not a substitute for B:
`TestListActiveApplications_MatchesTerminalApplicationStatuses` derives
its expected-included set from `TerminalApplicationStatuses()` itself
rather than hardcoding status names a second time in the test, and
creates one real application per every status in `ApplicationStatuses()`
-- so it stays a meaningful check even if the terminal set changes later,
rather than freezing today's two specific values into the test forever.

Verified: new tests for `TerminalApplicationStatuses()` itself (exact
value, and that every terminal status is a real known status); the new
drift-detection integration test; both pre-existing `ListActiveApplications`
tests unchanged and green against the real store; full build/test/lint/
fmt clean; `tui`'s call site (`app.go`) untouched and still green,
confirming the public API didn't change.

## 2026-09-01 -- Add Lever job board support (#31)
Third source, following the exact adapter pattern #30 (Greenhouse)
established: `sync.PostingFetcher` interface, a `lever` package mirroring
`ashby`/`greenhouse`'s shape, a `LeverFetcher` translation adapter, wired
into `cmd/swamp/main.go`'s `newSyncer` and the TUI's company-form source
picker (`companySources`, `sourceRefLabels`).

Verified Lever's actual public API against real live data before writing
any code, rather than assuming from docs (the issue itself flagged this
as unverified). `GET https://api.lever.co/v0/postings/{site}?mode=json`
-- found a real active board (`ro`, Ro/Roman Health) by probing several
candidate site slugs directly, then fetched its real JSON with curl.
Caught two concrete discrepancies between the docs summary and live
data: `workplaceType`'s real value is `"onsite"`, not `"on-site"` as the
docs description implied; `categories.department` is omitted from the
JSON entirely when unset, not sent as `""`. Also confirmed structural
facts the issue left open: the top-level response is a bare JSON array
(not `{"jobs": [...]}` like Ashby/Greenhouse), `createdAt` is
milliseconds since the Unix epoch (not seconds), and an unbounded
request (no `skip`/`limit`) returns everything in one call -- Ro's real
48 postings came back with no pagination needed, consistent with how
`ashby`/`greenhouse` are already implemented (single-request, no
pagination loop).

One implementation detail worth noting: Lever provides `descriptionPlain`
natively, unlike Greenhouse (HTML-only, required `stripHTML`, see
`greenhouse/html.go`) -- no HTML-stripping utility needed here at all.

`companySources` gained a third entry (`lever`, appended last, cycling
order ashby -> greenhouse -> lever). This changed two existing tests'
expected wrap-around values (`TestCompanyFormModel_RightArrow_...`,
`TestCompanyFormModel_LeftArrow_...` in `tui/company_form_test.go`) --
updated them to match 3 sources instead of 2, plus added a
Lever-specific creation test mirroring the existing Greenhouse one.

Verified: `lever` package unit tests against a hand-built fixture
(covering the confirmed missing-department behavior explicitly,
`RawPayload` preservation, non-OK status handling) plus a throwaway
verification program run directly against Ro's live board (not just the
fixture) confirming the real client decodes real dates/URLs/fields
correctly end to end. `sync.LeverFetcher` translation test. Manually
verified via tmux end to end against the real built binary: added a
company with source=lever through the actual TUI form (source picker
showed all three options, cycling worked, label switched to "Lever
site"), pressed 'r' and confirmed a real sync against Ro's live board
(48 fetched, 48 created), and opened a real posting's detail view
confirming team/location/employment type/workplace type/job URL/
application URL/plaintext description all render correctly with no HTML
leakage. Full build/test/lint/fmt clean.

## 2026-09-01 — Trim fetched posting fields before filter matching and persistence
Context: investigating a Stripe/StackAdapt data-quality question surfaced
that job board APIs (Greenhouse, Ashby) return free-text fields with
inconsistent leading/trailing whitespace -- e.g. Stripe's Greenhouse
listings had both "Dublin" and "Dublin " for the same city, and 35 of
its postings had padded `location` values. Tracing the ingestion path
(ashby/greenhouse/lever fetchers -> sync.Posting -> toCreatePostingParams
-> store) showed no trimming anywhere; every string field was a verbatim
passthrough from the source API's JSON.
Decision: added `sanitizePosting` in `sync/sanitize.go`, applied once in
`SyncCompany` immediately after `fetcher.FetchPostings` returns, trimming
every free-text `Posting` field (SourceID, Title, Department, Team,
Location, EmploymentType, WorkplaceType, DescriptionHTML, DescriptionText,
JobURL, ApplicationURL). `RawPayload` is deliberately left untouched
(it's the source's raw JSON, kept verbatim for audit) and `PublishedAt`
isn't a string.
Why: this is a single choke point that every source funnels through
before filter matching or persistence, so one function fixes it for all
three fetchers (and any future ones) rather than patching each
individually. Applying it before `filter.Match` matters as much as
applying it before save: `filter.Match` does an exact (case-insensitive)
comparison, so an untrimmed posting location like "Canada " silently
failed to match a clean company_filters value of "Canada" -- a real
matching bug, not just messy stored data. `company_filters` values
themselves didn't need a separate fix: the TUI populates them from
`ListDistinctDepartmentsForCompany`/`ListDistinctLocationsForCompany`,
which read from `postings` -- already-sanitized by this fix.
Verified: new table-driven unit test for `sanitizePosting` (trims every
field, leaves RawPayload/PublishedAt untouched). Two new
`sync.SyncCompany` integration tests: one confirming a padded fetched
posting is saved trimmed, one reproducing the filter-matching bug
directly (a "Canada" filter against a fetched "Canada " location) and
confirming it now matches. Full existing sync suite still green. Full
build/test/lint/fmt clean.
Alternatives considered: sanitizing inside each fetcher (ashby_fetcher.go,
greenhouse_fetcher.go, lever_fetcher.go) individually -- rejected as
triplicated logic with no shared enforcement if a fetcher is added later
and someone forgets the trim call. Sanitizing only in
`toCreatePostingParams` (right before save) -- rejected because it runs
after `filter.Match`, so it wouldn't have fixed the exact-match bug.
Did not backfill existing rows with padded values already in the
database; this fix only prevents new writes from being unsanitized.

## 2026-09-01 — Backfill existing posting rows with padded whitespace (migration 00005)
Context: the previous entry's `sync.sanitizePosting` fix only stops new
writes from being unsanitized. Rows already in the database (Stripe,
StackAdapt, etc.) still carry the padded whitespace found during
investigation.
Decision: added goose migration `00005_trim_existing_posting_whitespace`,
a pure data UPDATE (no schema change) that runs `TRIM()` over every
free-text `postings` column sanitizePosting also covers (source_id,
title, department, team, location, employment_type, workplace_type,
description_html, description_text, job_url, application_url).
`raw_payload` and `published_at` are left alone, same reasoning as the
Go-side fix. Down is a documented no-op: trimming discards the exact
original whitespace, so there's nothing to restore it from -- a
fabricated restoration would misrepresent what was actually stored.
Why: keeps the backfill in the same versioned, repeatable mechanism
(`task migrate:up`) every other schema/data change in this project goes
through, rather than a manual one-off SQL command against just the
current local db that leaves no trace and wouldn't apply anywhere else
(fresh clones, CI, another environment).
Verified: new migrations_test.go case migrates to version 4, inserts a
posting with padded values on every affected column plus untouched
raw_payload, migrates to version 5, and asserts every column trimmed
and raw_payload unchanged. Full migrations suite, full build/test/lint/
fmt clean.

## 2026-09-01 -- Unify posting-shape mapping via jobboard.Posting + store.IngestedFields (#57)
Context: Lever's addition (#31, previous entry) replicated the exact
duplication #57 tracked: four near-identical ~13-field shapes
(ashby.Posting, greenhouse.Posting, lever.Posting, sync.Posting) hand-
copied at every source-adapter and every store-mapping call site, with
no compiler check that a field added to one was added to the others.
Ran the full `/improve-codebase` design process: framed the problem
space, spawned 4 differently-constrained design agents in parallel
(minimal interface, maximum flexibility, optimize for the common
caller, ports & adapters), compared designs in prose, then posted an
RFC to issue #57 (github.com/dklassen/swamp/issues/57#issuecomment-5497117510)
before implementing.
Decision, two parts:
1. `jobboard.Posting` (new package, stdlib-only) is now the single
   canonical source-agnostic posting shape. `ashby.Posting`,
   `greenhouse.Posting`, `lever.Posting`, and `sync.Posting` are all
   `type X = jobboard.Posting` aliases, not independently-declared
   structs that happen to match -- a client's Posting IS sync's
   Posting, so every source client satisfies `sync.PostingFetcher`
   directly with zero adapter/translation code. Deleted the three
   ~13-field hand-copy adapter files this obsoleted
   (sync/{ashby,greenhouse,lever}_fetcher.go and their tests) and wired
   clients directly into `newSyncer` in cmd/swamp/main.go.
2. `store.IngestedFields` (embedded into both `store.Posting` and
   `store.CreatePostingParams`) is now the single place that defines
   "what counts as a posting's content" -- `sync.toIngestedFields`
   builds one `IngestedFields` value from a fetched Posting instead of
   13 separate field assignments, and `sync.postingContentChanged`
   collapses to `!cmp.Equal(existing.IngestedFields,
   params.IngestedFields)`, replacing the hand-written
   `stringPtrEqual`/`timePtrEqual` chain (both helpers deleted, now
   unused).
Explicitly out of scope (per the RFC, to keep this change reviewable):
`store.Posting`'s pointer-typed fields (`*string` where nothing
distinguishes nil from empty) -- filed separately as issue #67 rather
than folded in, since it's an independent, wider-blast-radius change
store doesn't need to make just to fix #57. A generic
`fieldmap.Mapper[S,D]` engine (one design agent's proposal) --
rejected as premature abstraction per this project's own stated
philosophy (CLAUDE.md: "three examples before extracting"; this is the
second real mapping-collapse in the codebase, not the third).
`postingFromRow`'s per-field `sql.NullString` unwrapping in
store/posting.go -- left as-is; it's already one place, not duplicated.
Discovered mid-implementation: embedding `IngestedFields` means every
existing call site constructing `store.Posting{...}` or
`CreatePostingParams{...}` with flat field keys (e.g. `Title: "x"`)
stopped compiling -- reading a promoted field works at any Go version,
but writing one via a flat keyed struct literal needs Go 1.27+, and
this project's go.mod declares go 1.26.1. Confirmed via the real
compiler, not just the LSP, before treating it as real. Fixed by
nesting those fields under an explicit `IngestedFields: ...` key at
every affected site (11 files: store/store_test.go,
store/posting_test.go, stage/stage_test.go, tui/tui_test.go,
tui/active_applications_test.go, tui/posting_list_test.go,
tui/posting_detail_test.go, plus sync/company.go itself) -- a
mechanical fix, not a design change.
Added `sync.TestIngestedFields_CoversEveryPostingField`
(sync/field_completeness_test.go), a reflection-based test comparing
`jobboard.Posting`'s field names (minus SourceID, which identifies a
posting rather than being ingested content) against
`store.IngestedFields`'s field names -- guards against a field being
added to one and silently forgotten on the other now that
`toIngestedFields` has no per-field code path left to miss. Lives in
`sync`, not `store`, since store deliberately has no knowledge of
jobboard (store shouldn't import a job-board-specific package).
Verified: full existing store/sync/tui suites still green after every
call site was updated (no test assertions changed, only construction
syntax). New completeness test passes and was confirmed to actually
catch drift by temporarily adding a field to Posting alone. Manually
verified via tmux against a seeded scratch db: company list -> posting
list (Title/Department/Location columns) -> posting detail (Department/
Location/Job URL/description text) all render correctly end to end,
confirming the embedded field flows through db row -> postingFromRow ->
TUI rendering with no regression. Full build/vet/test/lint/fmt clean.

## 2026-09-02 -- Drop the per-package Posting aliases from #57's Part 1
Context: reviewing #57 Part 1 raised whether `ashby.Posting`,
`greenhouse.Posting`, `lever.Posting`, and `sync.Posting` (each
`type Posting = jobboard.Posting`) were pulling their weight, or just
adding a name with no function.
Checked: none of the four packages attach any method or field to their
local alias -- every use is a bare return-type/parameter-type/composite
literal. The zero-adapter benefit the original decision cites (a source
client's Posting IS sync's Posting, so it satisfies `sync.PostingFetcher`
directly) holds purely because everyone ultimately names the same
underlying type; it doesn't depend on the local aliases existing.
Deleted all four `type Posting = jobboard.Posting` declarations;
`ashby`/`greenhouse`/`lever`/`sync` and their tests now reference
`jobboard.Posting` directly. Mechanical change only -- no behavior
differs, since an alias and its target were already identical in every
respect. Updated the doc comments in cmd/swamp/main.go, jobboard/posting.go,
and filter/filter.go that described the now-removed aliases.
Verified: full build/vet/test/lint/fmt clean, no test assertions changed
(construction syntax only).

## 2026-09-02 -- Add indexOfCompany, matching indexOfPosting (#40)
Context: tui/app.go had three independent inline "loop over a slice,
compare .ID, break" copies (companyDeletedMsg, companyNameUpdatedMsg,
postingMarkupUpdatedMsg's archive-removal branch), plus a fourth,
already-generalized version (indexOfPosting) that was typed to
[]store.Posting so couldn't cover the two company-list sites.
Decision: added indexOfCompany([]store.Company, id int64) int alongside
indexOfPosting, following the same shape, rather than a generic
indexByID[T any] -- matches this codebase's established style (no
generics used anywhere else) and was the option the issue itself flagged
as simpler. Replaced all three inline loops with calls to the appropriate
helper. Pure cleanup, no behavior change.
Verified: existing tests already covering all three call sites
(TestApp_PressD_DeletesSelectedCompany, TestApp_PressE_RenameKeepsCompanyListSorted,
TestApp_PressX_WhileHidingArchived_RemovesPostingFromViewAndClampsCursor)
pass unchanged. Manually verified via tmux against a seeded scratch db:
rename, delete, and archive-while-hiding all behave identically to
before. Full build/test/lint/fmt clean.

## 2026-09-02 -- Remove dead MarkPostingInterested/ArchivePosting (#58)
Context: store/posting_markup.go exposed 8 methods for two independent
booleans (interested/archived) plus notes. MarkPostingInterested and
ArchivePosting had zero production callers -- confirmed via grep across
the whole repo, only their own tests called them -- while every real
caller (tui/app.go's toggleInterested/toggleArchived) used the Set*
variants. The undocumented-in-the-name difference (SetPostingInterested
also clears ArchivedAt; MarkPostingInterested doesn't) was a silent
correctness footgun: picking the wrong one produces wrong markup state
with no compiler or obvious runtime error.
Decision: removed MarkPostingInterested and ArchivePosting (Store
methods, sqlc queries in db/queries/posting_markup.sql, and generated
store/db code via `task sqlc:generate`) rather than renaming to make the
"also clears" behavior explicit -- the issue's own preferred option,
since these two were genuinely unused rather than needed under a
clearer name. UnmarkPostingInterested/UnarchivePosting stay (real
production callers, the "toggle off" counterparts). UpdatePostingMarkupNotes
was also found to have zero production callers (posting-markup notes
have no TUI entry point -- only Application.Notes does) but is left
alone: out of scope, not something this issue flagged, and removing it
wasn't asked for.
TestListInterestedPostings_ExcludesArchived used ArchivePosting
specifically to construct "interested AND archived simultaneously set"
(a state the schema allows but, after this change, no longer reachable
through any public Store method) to guard ListInterestedPostings' WHERE
clause itself, not just today's TUI-reachable states. Rewrote it to set
archived_at via a direct SQL UPDATE against s.sqlDB (the test file is
package store, so this is in-package, not a new exported backdoor) so
the invariant stays tested rather than being silently narrowed to
"archived implies not interested" by construction.
Verified: full build/test/lint/fmt clean. Manually verified via tmux
against a seeded scratch db: interested toggle on/off, archive, and
unarchive all still work through the TUI exactly as before (no TUI code
changed).

## 2026-09-02 -- store.Posting's optional fields become plain string/time.Time (#67)
Context: store.IngestedFields' nine optional string fields and
PublishedAt were *string/*time.Time, mirroring the underlying
sql.NullString/sql.NullTime columns, even though nothing in the codebase
distinguished nil from empty -- every consumer called
derefOr(p.X, "")/equivalent, or (posting_detail.go, posting_list.go) did
a `!= nil && *x != ""` check that collapses to the same thing. Deferred
out of #57 (posting-shape mapping unification) as a separate,
wider-blast-radius decision.
Decision: dropped the pointer types. IngestedFields' string fields
become plain string (empty = absent), PublishedAt becomes plain
time.Time (zero = absent). postingFromRow now does direct field access
(row.Department.String, row.PublishedAt.Time) instead of 9 `if
row.X.Valid` branches, since sql.NullString{}/sql.NullTime{}'s own zero
values already are the correct absent-value. sync.toIngestedFields
collapses to a straight field-by-field copy (jobboard.Posting was
already plain-typed) -- stringOrNil is deleted, now unused.
nullString's signature changed (only ever called from posting.go) but
nullTime's did NOT: it's shared with InterviewStage.StageDate, a
genuinely pointer-optional field out of this issue's scope. Added a
separate nullPublishedAt(time.Time) for IngestedFields.PublishedAt
specifically rather than overloading nullTime's meaning across two
different nullability contracts.
Fixed every derefOr/`!= nil` call site this pointer removal broke:
tui/app.go (postingDetailContent's field list, description-text check,
splitCompanyFilters' filter.Posting conversion -- derefOr itself
deleted, now fully unused), tui/posting_list.go (department/location
columns, and a JobURL `!= nil && *url != ""` open-in-browser gate the
issue's own investigation hadn't listed), tui/posting_detail.go (the
JobURL open-in-browser gate the issue did list), sync/company_test.go
and store/posting_test.go (test fixtures constructing pointer values).
Real trade-off, not free (per the issue's own framing): this closes the
door on ever distinguishing "this source has no concept of Team" from
"this source has Team but it's genuinely blank" -- both collapse to ""
either way now. All evidence checked said that distinction was never
made use of.
Noted, not acted on: store.Posting is JSON-marshaled by stage.Candidate/
Prepared with no json: tags (see #59), so this changes the agent
hand-off JSON shape for these fields from `"department": null` (when
absent) to `"department": ""` -- always present now, never omitted/null.
No test asserted on the old shape (confirming #59's own finding: zero
structural safety net here). Left for #59 to address properly, not
folded in here.
Verified: full build/test/lint/fmt clean, no test assertions changed in
meaning (only pointer-literal construction syntax). Manually verified
via tmux against a seeded scratch db: a posting with every optional
field populated and one with none of them render identically to before
in both the list and detail views, and the JobURL open-in-browser gate
correctly no-ops on the empty-JobURL posting without crashing.

## 2026-09-03 -- Unify ingestion-time and display-time filter matching (#61)
Context: sync/company.go's toFilterRules/toFilterPosting (ingestion
gating) and tui/app.go's splitCompanyFilters+narrowPostingsToFilters
(display narrowing) both built []filter.Filter and called filter.Match,
independently. The real risk wasn't the per-posting match call (already
single-sourced via filter.Match) but the field-name strings
"department"/"location", hardcoded independently in 9 places across
filter/filter.go, tui/app.go, and tui/filter_select.go -- a new
filterable field needed all of them touched in lockstep with nothing to
catch a miss.
Decision, two parts:
1. Exported filter.FieldDepartment/FieldLocation constants; replaced all
   9 string-literal sites with them (filter/filter.go's own
   fieldAccessors map, tui/app.go's splitCompanyFilters/
   narrowPostingsToFilters/saveCompanyFilters, tui/filter_select.go's
   itemAtCursor/Update).
2. Exported sync.FilterRules (was toFilterRules) and added
   tui.filterPostingsByCompanyFilters, which calls it directly --
   tui/app.go's loadPostings (the authoritative DB-driven filtering
   pass) now shares the exact same store.CompanyFilter -> []filter.Filter
   conversion SyncCompany uses at ingestion time, instead of
   round-tripping through splitCompanyFilters's departments/locations
   split and rebuilding rules from that. splitCompanyFilters itself
   stays -- its department/location split is still needed for other TUI
   display state (the "Filtering: ..." summary line, pre-populating the
   filter-select screen's checkboxes), not just for matching.
narrowPostingsToFilters (the optimistic client-side narrow applied
immediately after saving a filter selection, before the background
re-sync completes) also now delegates to filterPostingsByCompanyFilters,
via synthetic store.CompanyFilter{Field, Value} values built from its
departments/locations input -- no DB read needed, since sync.FilterRules
only reads Field/Value off each row. (An earlier version of this entry
claimed this delegation would require a synchronous DB read and left
narrowPostingsToFilters as a third independent rule-construction copy;
that claim was wrong -- caught during code review, see the follow-up
note below.) It's still non-authoritative in the same sense as before --
companyRefreshedMsg reloads from the DB right after specifically
because this pass is approximate -- but it's no longer a second
hand-copy of the matching logic itself.
Real behavior change, in loadPostings' favor: previously,
splitCompanyFilters silently dropped any company_filters row whose
field wasn't "department"/"location" (its switch has no default), so
an unrecognized field was never applied and never errored --
filterPostingsByCompanyFilters now routes every row through
sync.FilterRules + filter.Match like ingestion does, so an unsupported
field surfaces as an error (propagated via postingsLoadedMsg{err:err}),
consistent with SyncCompany's existing behavior. Should be unreachable
given company_filters' CHECK constraint, but store/filter don't get to
assume that from outside their own package.
Added TestFilterPostingsByCompanyFilters_MatchSemantics (AND across
fields, OR within a field, matching SyncCompany's semantics) and
TestFilterPostingsByCompanyFilters_UnsupportedField_ReturnsError
(pins the behavior change above) directly against the new function,
rather than only indirectly through App-level tests.
Considered, rejected: running the full /improve-codebase design-agent
process the issue flagged as a candidate for -- the issue's own "why it
matters" was really about the scattered field-name strings and the two
independent rule-construction paths, not about filter.Match's algorithm
itself, so a smaller, directly-scoped fix addressed the actual risk
without the larger design-exploration overhead.
Verified: full build/vet/test/lint/fmt clean, all pre-existing filter
tests (sync.TestSyncCompany_*, tui.TestApp_*Filter*) pass unchanged.
Manually verified via tmux against a seeded scratch db: a pre-existing
saved department filter narrows the posting list correctly on open
(exercising the new loadPostings path), and switching to a different
filter and forcing a fresh reload (back out, re-enter) correctly picks
up postings the previous filter had excluded.

Follow-up (same day, before push): ran an 8-angle code review against
this change. Four independent angles (reuse, cross-file tracer,
simplification, altitude) converged on the same real gap: the initial
version left narrowPostingsToFilters as a third independent
rule-construction-and-match loop, justified in this log by a claim (see
above, now corrected) that unifying it would need a synchronous DB
read -- wrong, since sync.FilterRules only reads Field/Value. Fixed:
narrowPostingsToFilters now delegates to filterPostingsByCompanyFilters
via synthetic CompanyFilter rows, so there's exactly one
rule-construction-and-match implementation, not three. Also fixed:
tests that still used raw "department"/"location" string literals
instead of the new filter.FieldDepartment/FieldLocation constants
(tui/filter_select_test.go, tui/app_test.go) -- cheap, and the PR's own
premise was making the field name a single source of truth.
Considered and deliberately left as-is: filterPostingsByCompanyFilters
propagating a filter.Match error blanks the entire posting list
(postings/markup/active-filter badges all zeroed) rather than degrading
gracefully like the pre-#61 path did. Real severity increase in the
abstract, but confirmed unreachable in normal use (company_filters has
a CHECK (field IN ('department','location')) constraint the store API
can't bypass), and consistent with this project's existing fail-loudly
stance (filter.Match's own doc comment) -- softening it would mean
re-introducing silent partial-filtering, the exact failure mode #61 was
filed to eliminate elsewhere. Added
TestApp_PostingsLoadedMsgWithErr_ClearsPostingsAndMarkup to pin this
behavior directly (App.Update given postingsLoadedMsg{err:...}) rather
than leave it undocumented, since a full DB-level integration test
can't construct the trigger condition -- CreateCompanyFilter itself
would be rejected by the CHECK constraint before an unsupported-field
row could ever reach loadPostings.
Re-verified: full build/vet/test/lint/fmt clean after the above fixes.

## 2026-09-03 -- postings' optional TEXT columns become NOT NULL DEFAULT '' (#74)
Context: #67 dropped store.IngestedFields' *string/*time.Time pointer
types since nothing distinguished nil from empty at the Go layer. The DB
schema stayed nullable for the same nine TEXT columns, which meant the
possibility could leak back in at any time -- a new sqlc-generated field
on a nullable column defaults to sql.NullString, so the next person to
touch this area would have to rediscover "we don't distinguish nil from
empty here" as an unenforced convention instead of the schema simply
refusing the ambiguity. Filed as a follow-up rather than folded into #67,
since a schema migration on the live DB is a separate, real decision.
Asked (and answered, before implementing) whether this trades anything
away: checked SQLite's own record format -- a NULL is serial type 0 (zero
bytes of payload), an empty TEXT is serial type 13 (2*len+13 with len=0,
also zero bytes of payload), both fit the same single-byte varint in the
row header. No storage or performance difference either way; the only
thing NULL bought here was a semantic distinction nothing in this
codebase makes.
Decision: department, team, location, employment_type, workplace_type,
description_html, description_text, job_url, application_url all become
NOT NULL DEFAULT ''. published_at (TIMESTAMP) deliberately stays
nullable -- a NOT NULL timestamp needs a sentinel date (Go's zero value,
0001-01-01), which is worse than nullable for anything querying this
table outside Go ("WHERE published_at IS NOT NULL" is honest SQL,
"WHERE published_at != '0001-01-01 00:00:00'" is a footgun) --
nullPublishedAt (added in #67) already does the zero-time<->NULL
translation this boundary exists for.
Migration 00006 rebuilds postings (SQLite has no ALTER TABLE ... ALTER
COLUMN), same pattern 00002/00003/00004 used: CREATE postings_new with
the tightened schema, INSERT ... SELECT with COALESCE(col, '') on the
nine columns being backfilled, DROP + RENAME, then recreate
idx_postings_company_id/idx_postings_listing_status (DROP TABLE also
drops a table's indexes). No PRAGMA foreign_keys dance -- this project
has never turned FK enforcement on, and the pragma is a no-op
mid-transaction (which goose already wraps migrations in) regardless.
sqlc regenerated db.Posting/CreatePostingParams/UpdatePostingParams with
plain string for the nine columns (published_at stays sql.NullTime).
store/posting.go simplifies accordingly: postingFromRow does direct
field assignment instead of .String unwrapping, nullString is deleted
(no longer has any caller), and UpsertPosting's param construction drops
the wrapping calls. ListDistinctDepartmentsForCompany/
ListDistinctLocationsForCompany collapse to one-line passthroughs --
sqlc now generates []string directly instead of []sql.NullString once
the column can't be NULL, so the manual .Valid-filtering loop in each
became dead code. Also dropped the now-always-true "department IS NOT
NULL" clauses from db/queries/postings.sql -- redundant once the column
can't be NULL.
Verified: full build/vet/test/lint/fmt clean. Added three migration
tests (db/migrations/migrations_test.go): existing NULL values backfill
to '' on Up, an explicit NULL insert is rejected after Up (the
constraint is actually enforced, not just a one-time backfill), and
omitting the column entirely still succeeds via the DEFAULT. Manually
verified the Down migration round-trips cleanly (schema reverts,
indexes recreated) via `task migrate:up`/`migrate:down` against a
scratch db. Manually verified via tmux: seeded a scratch db at migration
version 5 with a NULL-department posting, launched the real binary
(which auto-migrates to 6 via goose.Up in main.go), confirmed via
`typeof()` in sqlite3 that the backfilled value is genuinely '' (not
NULL), and confirmed the posting list and detail view render identically
to before -- blank Department/Location, no crash.

## 2026-09-03 -- Document review tracking, point 1 (#51)
Context: #51 splits into three parts (spec, review cycle, metrics);
comment thread narrowed the first pass to "point 1" specifically: DB
tables for reviews (transactional, never overwritten, linked to a review
cycle), TUI to submit them, and how to link the agent that drafted a
document to its review. Discussed in chat before implementing (issue
itself calls for simplest-implementation-first, not full design up
front, but still asked rather than assumed on the load-bearing calls).
Key existing-code facts that shaped this: documents.Store overwrites
cover_letter.md/resume.md in place with zero versioning of its own, and
documents' own package doc comment states Swamp never reads document
content, only checks presence -- both had to be addressed head-on, not
worked around.
Decisions:
1. content_snapshot on every review row: without it, a review recorded
   today becomes unreadable once the file gets redrafted next week.
   Mirrors this project's own established pattern for "track history
   without losing it" (posting_history's full-snapshot approach, not a
   diff or pointer).
2. content_sha256 alongside content_snapshot (asked and confirmed in
   chat): computed once at write time from content already in hand, so
   "did this change between review N-1 and N" becomes a cheap indexed
   comparison instead of diffing TEXT blobs. Deliberately NOT a
   standalone hash tracked outside formal reviews (e.g. updated on every
   `stage prepare` or TUI visit) -- that's really the seed of the
   deferred "agent/user interaction event" tracking (comment 2's
   territory), out of scope for point 1.
3. How drafting links to review, resolved minimally: it doesn't, not
   yet. cycle is an auto-incrementing count of reviews per (application,
   document_type), computed by store.CreateDocumentReview (COUNT+1), not
   supplied by the caller or tied to a separately-instrumented "draft"
   event. The content_snapshot IS the record of what got reviewed; two
   consecutive reviews with identical snapshots are directly visible by
   comparing them (or their sha256) without Swamp needing to watch the
   filesystem or the skill needing to call a new subcommand after every
   write. Deferred, not decided against -- revisit once there's real
   review history to learn from.
4. document_type/outcome are plain TEXT with a DB CHECK constraint
   (cover_letter/resume, passed/flagged), not a Go-typed enum. Unlike
   applications.status (see 00004_drop_application_status_check_
   constraint.sql, PR #17 review), which moved to Go-only enforcement
   specifically because it already had significant schema churn and a
   pre-existing Go enum duplicating it, document_type/outcome are new,
   small, and stable -- interview_stages.outcome is the closer precedent
   (plain TEXT + CHECK, no Go enum), followed here instead.
5. documents package untouched -- its "never reads content" boundary
   stays true. The TUI reads file content directly via os.ReadFile once
   the user commits to reviewing a specific document
   (documentReviewSelectModel.Update), using the path documents.Store
   already resolves; store.CreateDocumentReview takes content as a
   plain string parameter and has no filesystem access of its own.
Schema: migration 00007 adds document_reviews (application_id FK,
document_type, cycle, content_snapshot, content_sha256, outcome, notes,
created_at), UNIQUE(application_id, document_type, cycle) as a safety
net behind the store-computed cycle. No updated_at -- reviews are
append-only, matching the issue's explicit "we don't overwrite reviews."
TUI: two new screens on the posting-detail flow ('r', gated on
hasApplication like 's'/'n'), mirroring existing shapes exactly rather
than inventing new ones -- documentReviewSelectModel is a cursor-list
picker like applicationStatusModel (shows Cover Letter/Resume with
found/not-found, Enter on a missing one no-ops), documentReviewFormModel
is a textarea like applicationNotesModel but with two submit bindings
instead of one (ctrl+p passed, ctrl+f flagged) rather than a separate
outcome-picker step -- avoids inventing a multi-field form shape with no
precedent in this codebase for what's fundamentally still "notes plus
which of two keys you press."
Explicitly deferred (comment 2's scope, not point 1): agent/session
interaction events, user-opened/user-edited tracking, and any Swamp-side
subcommand the skill would call mid-draft. Also deferred: surfacing
review history in postingDetailContent's view (nice-to-have, not needed
to start capturing reviews).
Verified: full build/vet/test/lint/fmt clean. New tests: store package
(cycle computation, per-document-type isolation, sha256, list ordering),
migrations package (CHECK constraints and UNIQUE constraint actually
enforced at the DB level, not just by well-behaved Go callers), tui
package (both new screen models' key handling, plus postingDetailModel's
new 'r' keybinding gated on hasApplication). Manually verified via tmux
against a seeded scratch db end to end: review-select shows correct
found/not-found status, Enter on a missing document no-ops, submitting
via ctrl+p/ctrl+f writes the expected row (content_sha256 populated,
notes captured), cycle increments correctly on a second review of the
same document type while staying independent across cover_letter vs.
resume, and esc/cancel from both new screens returns to posting-detail
without writing anything. Noted, not a regression: the review-form
screen's title can scroll off-screen in a full-height terminal --
confirmed this is pre-existing behavior shared by the notes-edit screen
(same textarea-sized-to-listRows() pattern), not something this change
introduced.

Follow-up (same day, before push): ran a 9-angle code review (the usual
8 plus an ad hoc SOLID-principles pass) against this change. Found and
fixed a real bug: ctrl+p/ctrl+f were chosen as the submit bindings
(passed/flagged) without checking against bubbles/textarea's own
DefaultKeyMap, which already binds ctrl+p to "previous line" and ctrl+f
to "character forward" -- pressing either while editing multi-line notes
would have silently submitted the review instead of moving the cursor.
Changed to ctrl+s (passed) and ctrl+g (flagged), both confirmed absent
from textarea's keymap; added a regression test that types two lines,
presses ctrl+p, and asserts no documentReviewCreatedMsg was produced.
Also fixed, found independently by 4+ of the 9 angles: postingID was
threaded dead through documentReviewSelectModel ->
enterDocumentReviewFormMsg -> documentReviewFormModel with no consumer
anywhere (removed from both models, the message, and their
constructors); documentReviewSelectModel also held an unused
*documents.Store field (only needed locally in its constructor, not
kept). Extracted renderCursorLine (tui/app.go, alongside the existing
renderFilterOption) since documentReviewSelectModel.View() was about to
become a fourth near-identical copy of the same cursor-highlight
rendering already duplicated in applicationStatusModel/company_list.go
-- used it in the new file only, left the three pre-existing copies
untouched (not this PR's job to retrofit working code). Fixed a real
documentation-accuracy gap: content_sha256's comment claimed it was
"indexed" when no index on it actually existed -- rather than walk back
the claim, added idx_document_reviews_content_sha256 (cheap, and makes
the documented intent true) instead of downgrading the comment.
Considered and left as-is: the CountDocumentReviews-then-INSERT two
round trips for cycle computation (SQLite single-writer, UNIQUE
constraint is the real safety net either way, and the two-step form
keeps cycle logic visible in Go next to its doc comment -- a defensible
readability choice per the angles that raised it, not a clear mistake);
the synchronous os.ReadFile in documentReviewSelectModel.Update (altitude
and conventions angles agreed this extends, rather than violates, the
existing tui/active_applications.go precedent for cheap local
filesystem I/O in Update -- added an explicit comment saying so, since
the prior version relied on the reader making that connection
unprompted); the hardcoded 2-document-type list in
newDocumentReviewSelectModel/documentTypeLabel (SOLID angle's OCP note
-- already the deliberate, documented choice from the original entry
above, not an oversight).
Re-verified: full build/vet/test/lint/fmt clean after the above fixes.
Manually re-verified the exact bug scenario via tmux: typed two lines of
notes, pressed ctrl+p, confirmed the screen stayed on the review form
with both lines intact (didn't submit), then pressed ctrl+s and
confirmed the review was recorded correctly (outcome=passed, both lines
in notes) and the screen returned to posting-detail.

## 2026-09-03 -- Pin the stage/CLI-to-agent JSON contract (#59)
Context: stage.Candidate/Prepared (embedding store.Posting) were
serialized by cmd/swamp's printJSON using Go's default reflect-based
field names -- no json: tags anywhere in store or stage. The actual
consumer is an external LLM agent following
.agents/skills/apply-to-posting/SKILL.md's documented JSON examples, not
Go code, so an ordinary field rename would silently break that hand-off
with nothing to catch it -- confirmed via a real `stage list`/`stage
prepare` run against seeded data before touching anything, matching
SKILL.md's documented shape exactly (PascalCase Go field names as JSON
keys).
Decision: added explicit json tags to store.IngestedFields/Posting and
stage.Candidate/Document/Prepared, each pinned to the exact name that
already serializes today -- not a redesign, a freeze. IngestedFields'
own embedding into Posting is deliberately left untagged so it stays
promoted/flattened (a tag there would nest it under an "IngestedFields"
key instead, silently changing the contract this change exists to
freeze). Verified byte-for-byte via a real `stage list` run before and
after: output identical except timestamps.
Added TestCandidate_JSONShape_MatchesDocumentedAgentContract and
TestPrepared_JSONShape_MatchesDocumentedAgentContract (stage/stage_test.go):
marshal a real Candidate/Prepared value, unmarshal into
map[string]json.RawMessage, and diff the sorted key set against the
expected one -- structural (field-name-set) pinning rather than a
literal golden string, since FirstSeenAt/CreatedAt/etc. are real
timestamps a golden string comparison would make flaky. Verified the
test actually catches drift by temporarily changing a json tag's value
(not the Go field, which would just be a compile error) and confirming
the test fails with a clear diff, then reverted.
Considered, not done as part of this issue: store.Application isn't
tagged, since it's never included wholesale in stage's JSON output
(only ApplicationID/ApplicationStatus are pulled out individually) --
out of this issue's actual blast radius.
Noticed, not fixed: PublishedAt (plain time.Time since #67, not
pointer) serializes as the literal string "0001-01-01T00:00:00Z" for
any posting with no known publish date, since Go's json encoder has no
built-in way to omit a zero-value struct field (omitempty doesn't
recognize a zero time.Time as empty) -- an agent reading this JSON sees
a nonsensical-looking date rather than an absent one. This is a real,
currently-shipping oddity, not introduced by this change, but this
change does pin it as part of the now-frozen contract. Flagged
separately rather than folded in here, since fixing it means a real
design decision (custom MarshalJSON on Posting, a stage-specific DTO
that omits/nulls it, or something else) with its own tradeoffs, not a
tag addition.
Verified: full build/vet/test/lint/fmt clean.

## 2026-09-03 -- Give company filter-edit its own module (#56)

Issue text flagged this as a candidate for improve-codebase's deeper
design pass, with no comments narrowing scope: the company filter-edit
workflow (department/location narrowing) had no single owning module --
persistence lived in store, resync lived in sync, and tui/app.go glued
the two together by hand across two separate async round trips (a fast
local save, then a separately-triggered slow resync) so the fast one's
completion message could drive an instant "optimistic narrow" of the
displayed postings before the slow one finished.

First design proposed: sync.Syncer.SaveCompanyFiltersAndSync(...), one
method doing persistence-then-resync as a single call. Rejected on
review: a method name joining two verbs with "And" is itself a SOLID
SRP smell, independent of whether the implementation internally does
the two things in sequence -- a name like that signals two reasons to
change bundled into one unit, not necessarily two badly-factored
implementations. Reworked into three cleanly separated pieces instead:

- store.ReplaceCompanyFilters(ctx, companyID, filters) -- pure
  persistence (delete-then-recreate the company's filter rows).
  Deliberately field-name-agnostic: only reads each element's
  Field/Value, so it carries no dependency on filter's
  FieldDepartment/FieldLocation constants -- store has zero import of
  the filter package anywhere in the codebase (confirmed via grep), and
  this keeps it that way. The caller (sync, which already imports both
  store and filter) decides what Field values mean.
- sync.ApplyCompanyFilters(ctx, companyID, departments, locations) --
  single verb, single responsibility: "changing a company's filters" as
  one cohesive unit. Internally calls ReplaceCompanyFilters then
  SyncCompany, but as a thin orchestrator with a name describing the one
  concept, not the two steps. The resync is not optional: SyncCompany
  gates ingestion itself through the company's filters (filter.Match),
  so postings that didn't match the OLD filter set were never stored at
  all -- narrowing what's already in the DB can't make a filter change
  fully take effect, only re-running ingestion under the new filters
  can.
- tui: filterSelectModel loses its *store.Store field entirely, becoming
  pure UI selection state with no I/O dependency of its own. Enter now
  returns a new saveFilterSelectionMsg{departments, locations} instead
  of directly kicking off the save. App.updateKeyMsg's
  screenFilterSelect case handles that message by narrowing the
  currently-displayed postings synchronously, in memory, immediately on
  keypress (before returning the Cmd that triggers
  sync.ApplyCompanyFilters), then separately returns that Cmd to persist
  and resync. This is a UX improvement over the original two-round-trip
  design, not just a refactor-neutral change: the optimistic narrow no
  longer waits on completion of even the fast local DB write, since it's
  now a pure computation over already-loaded data rather than a message
  handler reacting to a save having finished.

Verified: full build/vet/test/lint/fmt clean. New tests:
store.TestReplaceCompanyFilters_ReplacesExistingFiltersWithNewSet,
store.TestReplaceCompanyFilters_EmptySet_LeavesNoFilters,
sync.TestApplyCompanyFilters_ReplacesFiltersThenSyncs,
sync.TestApplyCompanyFilters_NoDepartmentsOrLocations_ClearsFilters;
tui's existing filter-select/app tests updated for the new
message-based, synchronous-narrow flow. Manually verified via tmux
against a seeded scratch db: pressing 'f' then toggling a department and
pressing Enter narrows the posting list instantly, before any network
round trip; the new filter row persists to company_filters even when the
subsequent resync fails (confirmed independently via direct sqlite3
query against a company pointed at a nonexistent job board -- proves
persistence commits before SyncCompany is attempted, and that the two
are correctly decoupled in terms of the write succeeding); the filter
survives a fresh, non-optimistic reload (back out and re-enter the
posting list); and esc/cancel from the filter-select screen leaves
company_filters completely unchanged (confirmed via sqlite3 query
before/after), i.e. cancel makes no DB writes.

Left untouched, deliberately: refreshCompany/companyRefreshedMsg in
tui/app.go, which still serve the independent manual 'r' refresh
keybinding on the company-list screen (tui/company_list.go) -- a
different, still-needed code path, not a leftover of the old
filter-save flow.

## 2026-09-03 -- Show latest document review outcome inline on posting detail (#83)

User asked for a way to see, at a glance, whether a drafted document
(cover letter/resume) last passed or got flagged in review, and why --
previously the only way to see a review's outcome was to walk through
the review-select/review-form screens again. Explicitly ruled out a
numeric score field ("don't see the value for a numeric scale unless we
were doing more complex review work") -- store.DocumentReview's existing
pass/flag Outcome + free-text Notes already carry everything needed;
this is a read-path addition, not a data-model change. Also explicitly
asked for the indicator per-document (cover letter/resume can fail
independently) and folded inline into the existing posting-detail
screen, not a separate screen -- so the "Documents" section postingDetailContent
already renders was the natural place, not a new screen alongside
review-select/review-form.

Added store.Store.LatestDocumentReview(ctx, applicationID, documentType)
-- thin wrapper over the existing ListDocumentReviews (already ordered
most-recent-cycle-first), returning (DocumentReview, ok, err) rather than
a slice, since every caller here only ever wants the latest one.

On the TUI side, postingDetailContent's own doc comment already states
the rule this had to follow: application/hasApplication are passed in
rather than fetched inline, specifically so store-backed state goes
through the same async tea.Cmd/tea.Msg round trip as the rest of the
screen (docs.Status via os.Stat is the one deliberate exception, since
that's local filesystem-existence-checking, not a DB read). So the
latest-reviews-per-document-type data follows loadApplication's own
pattern: loadDocumentReviews/documentReviewsLoadedMsg, mirrored via a
new postingDetailModel.latestReviews map[string]store.DocumentReview
field threaded through newPostingDetailModel and postingDetailContent.
A maybeLoadDocumentReviews(s, hasApp, applicationID) helper centralizes
"only fire the load when there's actually an application" across the
half-dozen places a.postingDetail's underlying application changes
(entering the screen, navigating prev/next, and every
application-mutation message handler) -- reload-rather-than-patch,
matching applicationStatusUpdatedMsg's existing "reload active
applications wholesale" precedent elsewhere in the same file.

documentReviewCreatedMsg's handler previously returned to
screenPostingDetail without rebuilding a.postingDetail at all -- a
pre-existing gap (harmless before, since nothing on that screen depended
on review state) that would have meant a freshly-submitted review's
outcome not showing until navigating away and back. Fixed as part of
this change: it now rebuilds and re-triggers the reviews load, same as
every other application-state transition.

Uncovered along the way: the tui test suite's applyCmd helper (used by
newTestApp for App.Init's tea.Batch, and now needed by
openPostingDetail too) ran app.Update(msg) but discarded the Cmd that
call itself returned -- fine when no handler chained a further Cmd, but
this change's loadApplication -> applicationLoadedMsg ->
loadDocumentReviews chain needs exactly that second hop. Fixed applyCmd
to recursively chase whatever Cmd each Update call returns, which is
what a real tea.Program's runtime loop does anyway -- not a workaround,
a correctness fix to the test harness. openPostingDetail (used by ~30
existing tests) switched from a raw sendKey(app, cmd()) to applyCmd for
the same reason: entering posting detail for an application that
already has one dispatches a tea.Batch(loadApplication,
maybeLoadDocumentReviews) once hasApp is known synchronously (e.g.
re-entering a posting already in a.applicationsByPosting), and a raw
sendKey can't unpack a BatchMsg.

Verified: full build/test/lint/fmt clean. New tests:
store.TestLatestDocumentReview_NoReviews_ReturnsNotFound,
store.TestLatestDocumentReview_ReturnsMostRecentCycle,
tui.TestPostingDetailModel_New_NoReviewsYet_ShowsNotReviewedBadge,
tui.TestPostingDetailModel_New_WithReviews_ShowsOutcomeAndNotes,
tui.TestApp_PostingDetail_DocumentHasReview_ShowsOutcomeAndNotesInline,
tui.TestApp_SubmitDocumentReview_ShowsImmediatelyOnPostingDetail.
Manually verified via tmux against a seeded scratch db (SWAMP_DB_PATH
and SWAMP_DOCUMENTS_PATH both pointed at scratch paths, to avoid
touching the real project's assets/db): an application with a flagged
cover-letter review and a passed resume review showed "[FLAGGED]" with
its notes line and "[PASSED]" inline on the posting-detail screen; a
second application with no reviews yet showed "[not reviewed]" for both
documents; and submitting a fresh review (r -> select cover letter ->
