## 2026-09-03 -- document_reviews enums move from DB CHECK to Go (#85)

User, reviewing the #83 work: document_reviews.document_type/outcome
should live as Go enums, matching applications.status, not as DB CHECK
constraints -- validation belongs at the boundary layer (where a raw row
becomes a store.DocumentReview), not duplicated in SQL. This directly
reverses 00007's own stated reasoning ("a small, stable set with no
history of churn, so it follows interview_stages.outcome's pattern
instead [of applications.status's]") -- overruled by the user now that a
second, independent use has appeared (see decisions.log's #83 entries
above): what looked stable in isolation wasn't once review UI grew a
compact-glyph rendering path alongside the full-badge one, i.e. exactly
the kind of use that benefits from a typed enum's compiler-checked
exhaustiveness over bare string comparisons.

store.ApplicationStatus (application_status.go) is the exact template,
copied faithfully: DocumentType and ReviewOutcome are now typed int
enums in store/document_review.go, each with a String()
(also the DB-persisted form), a Parse* that fails loudly on an unknown
value, and a Names array both go through so String/Parse can't drift
from each other. DocumentReview.DocumentType/Outcome are now these typed
fields, not string; documentReviewFromRow (renamed to return an error,
like applicationFromRow) is where an unknown DB value is now caught --
CreateDocumentReview/LatestDocumentReview/LatestDocumentReviews/
ListDocumentReviews all take/return the typed enums, calling .String()
only at the sqlc boundary. No MarshalJSON added (unlike
ApplicationStatus, which needed one for the stage package's CLI JSON
output) -- nothing serializes a DocumentReview to JSON yet, so that's
speculative until an actual caller needs it.

New migration 00008 drops document_type/outcome's CHECK constraints,
mirroring 00004's table-rebuild approach (SQLite has no ALTER TABLE ...
DROP CONSTRAINT) -- unlike 00004's table, document_reviews has its own
two indexes, so the rebuild recreates those explicitly or they'd be
silently lost. Down migration restores both CHECK constraints, same
"fail loudly if a row now violates them" reasoning as 00004's down. Left
00007's own comment/reasoning untouched rather than editing it
retroactively -- it accurately described the decision at the time it was
made; the reversal and its reasoning belong here and in 00008's own
comment, not rewritten into history.

The type change cascades through every caller: tui/document_review_
select.go's documentReviewOption.documentType, document_review_form.go's
documentReviewFormModel.documentType/documentTypeLabel/
createDocumentReview, and every map[string]store.DocumentReview (posting
detail's latestReviews, active-applications' ApplicationView.
LatestReviews, reviewGlyphSummary) all became map[store.DocumentType]... instead
-- caught one real bug along the way: reviewBadge's default case was
doing "[" + review.Outcome + "]" string concatenation, which stopped
compiling once Outcome became a typed ReviewOutcome (fixed with
.String()) -- a compile error, not a runtime one, which is exactly the
"validated at the boundary, not scattered as string comparisons
everywhere" property the user asked for actually paying for itself
immediately.

Explicitly left untouched: interview_stages.outcome, still a DB
CHECK-constrained plain string -- the user scoped this to document_
reviews specifically, and interview_stages wasn't part of the request.

Verified: full build/test/lint/fmt clean, `task sqlc:generate` produces
no diff (schema change doesn't affect sqlc's generated Go types, both
columns were already TEXT). New migration tests:
TestDropDocumentReviewsCheckConstraints_PreservesExistingRow,
TestDropDocumentReviewsCheckConstraints_ArbitraryValuesAccepted,
TestDropDocumentReviewsCheckConstraints_UniqueConstraintStillEnforced
(the last a regression check that the table rebuild didn't lose the
UNIQUE(application_id, document_type, cycle) constraint along with the
CHECK constraints). TestDocumentReviewsCheckConstraints_RejectInvalidValues
(00007's own test) needed no changes -- already pinned to migrateTo(t, 7),
so it continues to document the pre-00008 behavior correctly. Manually
verified via tmux against a fresh scratch db seeded through store.
CreateDocumentReview (typed enums end to end, not raw SQL): a seeded
flagged review round-tripped and displayed correctly on the active-
applications screen, and submitting a fresh review live through the TUI
(R -> select -> ctrl+s) persisted and updated the glyph as expected.

## 2026-09-03 -- Add MarshalJSON to DocumentType/ReviewOutcome preemptively (#85 follow-up)

Question raised in review of the above: since document_type/outcome are
now Go enums with no DB CHECK constraint, what's the risk of a future
reorder of the const block silently reinterpreting old data? Answer:
none for the DB path -- String()/ParseDocumentType (and the
ReviewOutcome equivalents) round-trip through the string form, and
documentTypeNames/reviewOutcomeNames are keyed array literals (indexed
by the constant itself, e.g. documentTypeNames: {DocumentTypeCoverLetter:
"cover_letter", ...}), so a reorder can't desync the name a constant
resolves to from what it resolved to before -- String() and Parse* stay
mutually consistent regardless of each constant's underlying int value.

The one real gap: neither type had a MarshalJSON, so Go's default
json.Marshal on a bare `type DocumentType int` emits the raw int, not
the string -- exactly like ApplicationStatus would without its own
MarshalJSON (see application_status.go, added for the stage package's
CLI JSON output). Nothing currently serializes a DocumentReview to
JSON, so this wasn't yet a live bug, but any int written to a durable
JSON blob (a log line, a cache, an external system) before some future
reorder would silently resolve to the wrong value when read back after
it, with no way to tell from the blob alone. Added MarshalJSON to both
types now, matching ApplicationStatus's exact implementation (marshal
String()'s output, not the int) -- cheap, and closes the gap before
there's any real data depending on the current ordering to go stale.

Verified: full build/test/lint/fmt clean. New tests:
store.TestDocumentType_MarshalJSON_UsesDBStringNotIntValue,
store.TestReviewOutcome_MarshalJSON_UsesDBStringNotIntValue. No manual
TUI verification -- this is a store-only addition with no runtime-visible
behavior change (nothing calls MarshalJSON on either type yet).

